        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Not long ago, many people predicted that public libraries would disappear. With information only a click away, why would anyone need a building full of books? Yet libraries have shown a remarkable ability to <b>adapt</b>. Instead of closing their doors, many now offer free internet access, computer classes and quiet places to study, and visitor numbers in some towns have actually risen.\n\nOne reason for this success is that libraries serve people whom the internet leaves behind. Not every household owns a computer, and not every family can afford a reliable connection. For these residents, the library is often the only place where they can apply for jobs online, complete school assignments or read the news. <u>In this way, libraries help to narrow the gap between those who are connected and those who are not.</u>\n\nLibraries also play an important social role. [I] In many neighbourhoods, they host reading groups, children's story hours and talks by local writers. [II] Such events bring together people of different ages and backgrounds who might otherwise never meet. [III] Elderly visitors, for example, often say that a weekly club is the highlight of their week. [IV] A library, in other words, is as much a community centre as it is a collection of books.\n\nOf course, libraries cannot rely on praise alone. Their future depends on whether governments continue to fund them and on whether they can keep responding to what residents actually need. If both conditions are met, there is little reason to believe that the library will vanish. If <b>they</b> are not, many communities may lose a service that no other institution can easily replace."
