        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "When people think of pollution, they usually picture smoke rising from factories or plastic floating in the sea. Noise, however, is also a form of pollution, and in modern cities it is almost impossible to escape. Traffic, construction work, aircraft and even loud music from shops fill the air from morning until night, so that many residents no longer notice how loud their surroundings have become.\n\nYet the body does notice. Long-term exposure to loud noise has been linked to difficulty sleeping, raised stress levels and problems with concentration. Children seem especially vulnerable: <b>those</b> who study in noisy classrooms often find it harder to follow lessons and to remember what they have learned. <u>Constant background noise therefore takes a quiet but real toll on people's health and learning.</u>\n\nFortunately, there are practical ways to reduce the problem. Cities can build barriers along busy roads, plant more trees and set limits on night-time construction. Architects can design buildings with thicker windows and walls. Individuals can also help by using headphones at a sensible volume and by choosing quiet spaces for rest. Although none of these measures is <b>costly</b> on its own, together they can make streets noticeably calmer.\n\nSome experts warn, however, that it would be a mistake to treat all sound as an enemy. Natural sounds such as birdsong, running water and rustling leaves seem to calm many listeners, and even busy cities can include <b>tranquil</b> places where such sounds can be heard. The goal, in other words, is not complete silence but a healthier balance of sounds."
