        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "In many Vietnamese homes, the evening meal has long been the centre of family life. Everyone gathers around the same table, shares dishes and talks about the events of the day. This daily ritual may seem <b>ordinary</b>, yet it plays an important part in keeping family members close.\n\nUnfortunately, modern schedules make such meals harder to arrange. Parents often come home late from work, and children stay at school for extra classes or spend their evenings in front of screens. <u>As a result, many families eat at different times, and the opportunity to talk with one another is gradually lost.</u> Some teenagers admit that they can go several days without having a real conversation with their parents.\n\nExperts, however, believe that the situation can be improved. [I] They suggest that families choose at least one meal a week that everyone must attend. [II] During this time, phones should be switched off so that people can give each other their full attention. [III] Children can be asked to help with cooking, which makes them feel valued. [IV] Over time, these small rules can turn into happy traditions.\n\nOf course, no family is perfect, and traditions cannot solve every problem. Yet parents who make an effort to listen often discover that their children are more willing to talk about worries at school or with friends. When teenagers feel understood, <b>they</b> are less likely to hide their feelings or to turn to strangers online. In this way, a shared meal can become a small but powerful way of building trust."
