        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Families around the world are becoming smaller. In many cities, couples now choose to have only one child because of the high cost of housing, education and healthcare. As a result, a growing number of children are growing up without brothers or sisters, and people often wonder how this experience affects them.\n\nFor a long time, only children were thought to be spoiled and lonely. Recent research, however, paints a different picture. Only children often perform as well in school as, or better than, <b>those</b> from larger families, partly because their parents can devote more time and money to their education. <u>What they may lack in sibling companionship, they frequently make up for through close relationships with adults and friends.</u> Many also develop strong imagination and independence.\n\nEven so, being an only child is not without difficulties. Some feel <b>considerable</b> pressure to meet their parents' high expectations, since all hopes rest on a single person. Others find it hard to learn to share or to settle disagreements, skills that brothers and sisters usually practise every day. Parents can help by encouraging their child to join clubs and teams, where such skills are learned naturally.\n\nIn the end, it is the quality of family life, not the number of children, that matters most. A loving home, open communication and opportunities to meet other people are what children of every family type need. Parents who provide these things can feel <b>confident</b> that their child will grow up happy and well-adjusted."
