        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "In Viet Nam, it is still common for three generations to live under the same roof. Grandparents help to look after the grandchildren, parents go out to work, and children grow up surrounded by relatives. This lifestyle was once <b>typical</b>, but in recent years it has become less common in big cities, where young couples increasingly prefer to rent their own apartments.\n\nLiving together has clear advantages. Grandparents can pass on family stories, traditional recipes and good manners, while children give them company and a sense of purpose. Parents also save money on childcare. <u>Nevertheless, sharing a home with several generations requires patience, because people of different ages often hold different views about how a household should be run.</u> Disagreements about housework, meals or discipline are common.\n\nFamilies who manage well usually follow a few simple principles. [I] They set clear rules about who does what, and they hold regular talks so that small problems do not grow. [II] Each person is also given some private space, even if it is only a corner of a room. [III] Above all, they show respect for one another's habits. [IV] These habits may differ widely, but a respectful attitude keeps the peace.\n\nSome young couples worry that they will lose their independence, and <b>they</b> have a point. Yet many who live apart later admit that they miss the support that older relatives provide. Perhaps the best solution is a compromise: living close to grandparents, rather than with them, allows families to enjoy both closeness and privacy."
