        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "Smartphones, tablets and televisions have become part of everyday family life. Parents often complain that their children spend too many hours looking at screens, while teenagers reply that their parents are just as busy with their own devices. The result is a daily struggle over who may use what, and for how long.\n\nResearch suggests that the problem is not screens themselves but the way they are used. Children who watch educational programmes or video-call their relatives gain more than <b>those</b> who spend hours scrolling aimlessly through videos. <u>What matters, in other words, is the quality of screen time rather than the number of hours.</u> Even so, too much of it can still cut into sleep, exercise and conversation.\n\nMany families have found simple ways of keeping a balance. Some switch off all devices during meals; others keep phones out of the bedroom at night. A few have created a \"family media plan\", in which parents and children together decide when and where screens may be used. Such a plan is usually <b>flexible</b>, changing as children grow older and their needs change.\n\nParents also have a part to play. Children learn by watching, so adults who put down their phones during conversations send a powerful message. Some experts <b>advocate</b> a \"tech-free hour\" each evening for the whole family, when everyone plays board games, cooks or simply talks. Such moments cost nothing, yet they strengthen the bonds that screens can so easily weaken."
