        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "Tet, the Lunar New Year, is the most important family festival in Viet Nam. Weeks before the holiday, households begin to prepare. They clean their houses, repaint the walls, and buy flowers and new clothes. These preparations are not simply chores; they express the hope for a <b>prosperous</b> new year.\n\nOne of the best-loved traditions is making banh chung, a square cake of sticky rice, pork and beans wrapped in leaves. The task takes many hours, so family members work together: some wash the leaves, others prepare the filling, and the children are often allowed to help tie the string. <u>While the cakes are boiling through the night, relatives sit around the fire, sharing stories and memories.</u> For many people, these quiet hours are the most precious part of the whole festival.\n\n[I] On the first days of Tet, families visit one another to exchange good wishes. [II] Younger members greet their elders and receive red envelopes containing lucky money. [III] In return, they promise to study hard and take good care of their parents and grandparents. [IV] The exchange teaches children to respect older people and to value family ties.\n\nIn recent years, however, some people have begun to question whether the traditional customs are still necessary. Busy parents may buy ready-made cakes, and some families spend the holiday travelling abroad instead of staying at home. Even so, many still try to keep at least a few customs alive, because <b>they</b> give the festival its special meaning and connect the generations."
