        "passage": "Almost everyone who grew up with brothers or sisters can remember quarrels over toys, food or a place on the sofa. Psychologists call this competition sibling rivalry, and they say that it is one of the most common sources of conflict in family life. Parents often find it tiring, and some fear that it means their children do not love each other.\n\nIn fact, rivalry usually shows that children are learning to share attention. A child may feel threatened when a new baby arrives, because love and care must now be shared. Studies also show that siblings who are close in age quarrel more often than <b>those</b> who are several years apart, probably because they want the same toys, friends and attention. <u>Quarrels of this kind are normal and, if handled well, can even teach children useful social skills.</u>\n\nHandled well, rivalry can be turned into something positive. Children who learn to negotiate, compromise and apologise at home find it easier to make friends at school. Parents can help by avoiding comparisons, giving each child some individual time and praising cooperation rather than <b>victory</b>.\n\nNot all rivalry disappears with age. Some adult siblings still compete over careers, money or their parents' attention. Yet many say that their relationship has become closer over the years, since shared memories of childhood create a bond that friends cannot easily replace. Perhaps this is why, despite all the early quarrels, most people count their brothers and sisters among the most <b>enduring</b> relationships of their lives."
