        "answer": 0,
        "passage": "In the past, housework in many Vietnamese families was seen as women's work. Mothers cooked, cleaned and washed clothes, while fathers earned money and sons were rarely asked to help. Today, however, this <b>traditional</b> pattern is slowly changing, as more women work outside the home and more families recognise that fairness matters.\n\nSurveys show that children who see both parents doing housework are more likely to share chores themselves. <u>Boys who grow up helping in the kitchen tend to become men who regard housework as a shared duty rather than a favour done for women.</u> Girls, in turn, learn that their time is just as valuable as their brothers'. For these reasons, many teachers encourage parents to give sons and daughters the same tasks.\n\nOf course, change does not happen overnight. [I] Some grandparents still expect girls to serve meals while boys relax, and parents may hesitate to argue with them. [II] Young people can respectfully explain why sharing chores benefits everyone. [III] When elders see how well the new system works, they often come to approve of it. [IV] Over time, such small victories help to change the attitudes of a whole family.\n\nPractical tools can make sharing easier. A weekly chart that lists tasks and who does them removes arguments about whose turn it is, and rotating the tasks prevents family members from feeling that <b>they</b> have been given the worst job. Over time, such routines become habits, and the home becomes a place where every member contributes and every contribution is valued."
