        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Millions of Vietnamese people live far from their hometowns, working in factories or studying in other cities and countries. For these families, distance is a constant challenge. A mother may spend years without seeing her son's face, and grandparents may watch their grandchildren grow up only through photographs.\n\nTechnology has made the separation easier to bear. Families who talk by video every week tend to feel closer than <b>those</b> who rely on the occasional phone call. <u>A ten-minute call may seem small, but it can give both sides the feeling that they still take part in each other's daily lives.</u> Messaging apps also let family members share photos and jokes during the day.\n\nHowever, screens cannot replace real presence. Grandparents who are unfamiliar with technology may feel <b>excluded</b>, and small children can lose interest after a few minutes. Time differences can also make it hard to find a moment when everyone is free. As a result, families often have to be creative, scheduling calls around meals or holidays and sending handwritten cards to mark special days.\n\nPerhaps the most important thing is to keep the connection <b>genuine</b>. Instead of asking only about work and grades, family members can share small details of ordinary life: what they cooked, what made them laugh, what worried them. Such details turn a call into a conversation, and a conversation into a bond that distance cannot easily break."
