        "passage": "More and more families in Vietnamese cities are welcoming a dog or a cat into their homes. For many people, a pet is no longer just an animal but a member of the family, with its own bed, favourite food and even birthday celebrations. This change reflects both rising incomes and a growing wish for <b>companionship</b> in a busy modern life.\n\nPets bring clear benefits, especially for children. A child who feeds and walks a dog every day learns responsibility and regular habits. <u>Many parents also report that their children become calmer and more caring after the arrival of a pet.</u> Elderly people, too, often find that a cat or dog gives them a reason to get up in the morning and keeps them active.\n\n[I] Yet keeping a pet is a long-term commitment. [II] Food, vaccinations and visits to the vet can be expensive, and a dog needs exercise every day, whatever the weather. [III] Some families also discover too late that a family member is allergic to animal hair. [IV] For this reason, experts advise families to think carefully and to spend time with the animal before they decide.\n\nFor families who are not ready to keep an animal, there are alternatives. Volunteering at a shelter, for example, allows children to enjoy the company of animals without the long-term responsibility. Some schools have also invited therapy dogs, which visit classrooms and help anxious pupils relax. Whatever the choice, the lesson is the same: caring for animals teaches us kindness, and <b>they</b> repay us with loyalty and affection."
