        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "Many people know their grandparents' names but little else about them. Where were they born? What did they do when they were young? Such questions often remain unanswered until it is too late, because the relatives who could answer them are no longer there to ask.\n\nToday, however, more and more families are making a deliberate effort to record their history. Some interview older relatives and write down their stories, while others collect old photographs and letters. Teenagers who take part in such projects often discover that they have more in common with <b>those</b> who lived before them than they expected. <u>Learning that a great-grandfather once failed an important exam, for instance, can make a young person's own mistakes seem less frightening.</u>\n\nCollecting family history requires patience. Memories are often incomplete or inconsistent, and relatives may remember the same event in <b>contrasting</b> ways. Researchers therefore advise comparing several sources, such as certificates, newspapers and photographs, before accepting a story as fact. Even so, a slightly different version does not make a story worthless; it may tell us how the family wished to remember itself.\n\nThe value of such projects goes beyond facts. A family that knows its past tends to feel more united, and children who know how earlier generations faced hardship may <b>cope</b> better with their own difficulties. A notebook of family stories, passed from one generation to the next, can therefore become a gift that no shop could sell."
