        "answer": 0,
        "passage": "The term 'sandwich generation' refers to adults who find themselves caring simultaneously for their own children and their ageing parents. Once regarded as a rare circumstance, it has become an increasingly <b>prevalent</b> pattern in many societies as people marry later, have children later, and life expectancy continues to rise. A person in their forties or fifties may therefore spend a significant portion of each week managing school pick-ups on one hand and medical appointments for a parent on the other.\n\nDespite the obvious strain, many people in this position report a surprising sense of purpose. Being needed by two generations at once can deepen family bonds and give caregivers a renewed appreciation for the sacrifices their own parents once made for them. <u>Several caregivers have noted that the experience, however exhausting, has brought them closer to relatives they previously saw only a few times a year.</u> Sharing responsibilities with siblings, rather than shouldering them alone, appears to make the biggest difference to how manageable the role feels.\n\n[I] Nevertheless, the pressures should not be underestimated. [II] Many caregivers report missing work deadlines, cancelling personal plans, and neglecting their own health in order to meet competing demands. [III] Financial strain is common too, since caring for an elderly parent may involve costly medical equipment or a reduction in working hours. [IV] Counsellors warn that, left unaddressed, this combination of emotional, physical and financial pressure can eventually lead to burnout.\n\nExperts who study family dynamics generally agree that no single solution works for everyone, but certain habits consistently help. Caregivers who schedule regular respite time, however brief, tend to cope noticeably better than <b>those</b> who attempt to manage everything without a break. Open conversations with employers about flexible hours, combined with support from siblings and community services, can transform an overwhelming burden into a shared responsibility. Ultimately, acknowledging the difficulty of the role, rather than silently enduring it, is often the first step towards finding genuine support."
