        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "For much of the twentieth century, the nuclear family – parents and their children living alone together – was considered the standard household in many Western countries. Grandparents, if mentioned at all, were pictured visiting on holidays rather than sharing a roof. In the past two decades, however, this picture has begun to shift, and multigenerational households, where grandparents, parents and children live under one roof, are becoming noticeably more <b>common</b> once again.\n\nEconomic pressure is one obvious driver. Rising housing costs have made it difficult for young couples to afford a home of their own, while some older parents find it increasingly hard to manage alone on a pension. <u>Pooling resources under a single roof, many families have discovered, often turns out to be more practical than maintaining two separate households.</u> Yet money is rarely the only motive: cultural traditions in many communities have long favoured several generations living together, and some families are simply returning to a pattern their own grandparents once followed.\n\nThe arrangement brings genuine advantages. Grandparents who live with their grandchildren can provide <b>affordable</b> childcare that is built on trust, while parents are freed to work longer hours or pursue further study. Children, meanwhile, often develop close relationships with grandparents and absorb family stories and traditions that might otherwise be lost. Grandparents themselves frequently report feeling less isolated and more purposeful than those who live entirely on their own.\n\nStill, no household arrangement suits everyone. Privacy can be harder to come by, and disagreements over parenting styles or household rules are common complaints. Couples may also feel they have less independence than friends who live apart from their relatives. Even so, surveys in several countries suggest that most adults in multigenerational households regard the arrangement positively overall, citing companionship and mutual support as benefits that outweigh the occasional friction."
