        "passage": "Long before anyone wrote a single word down, families preserved their history through stories passed from one generation to the next. A grandmother's account of surviving a difficult harvest, or a grandfather's memory of meeting his future wife, carries details that no official document could ever record. Psychologists who study memory argue that such stories are far from <b>trivial</b>; they shape how children come to understand their own identity.\n\nResearch conducted with adolescents has produced a striking pattern. Teenagers who know a reasonable amount about their family's history, including both its triumphs and its struggles, tend to show greater emotional resilience than those who know very little. <u>Knowing that relatives before them had overcome hardship appeared to give these young people a quiet sense that they, too, could cope with difficulty.</u> Family stories, in other words, can function as a kind of inherited confidence.\n\n[I] Despite this value, such stories are at real risk of disappearing. [II] Many grandparents who hold decades of family memory are elderly, and once they pass away, their stories often go with them. [III] Modern families, scattered across cities or even countries, increasingly have fewer opportunities for the long, unhurried conversations in which such stories are usually shared. [IV] A single missed conversation, repeated across a family for a generation or two, can mean that an entire chapter of family history is lost forever.\n\nFortunately, preserving these stories need not be difficult. Recording a grandparent's voice on a smartphone, or simply asking specific questions at a family gathering, can capture memories that would otherwise never be written down. Schools in several countries have begun encouraging students to interview older relatives as a classroom project, turning family history into a shared, living record rather than a fading memory. Such small efforts, researchers suggest, do far more than preserve the past; <b>they</b> strengthen the sense of belonging that every family member carries forward."
