        "answer": 0,
        "passage": "In almost every kitchen, there exists at least one dish that only a grandmother seems able to make exactly right. The exact proportions are rarely written anywhere; instead, they live in muscle memory, built through decades of repetition. As more families begin to notice how <b>fragile</b> this knowledge is, a quiet movement has emerged to write these unrecorded recipes down before they disappear.\n\nThe challenge is not simply culinary but emotional. <u>Grandmothers measuring in handfuls rather than grams often say that a recipe just needs to taste right, a standard that resists translation into neat written instructions.</u> Younger relatives attempting to record these dishes frequently report dozens of failed attempts, each one a reminder of how much skill lies behind a dish that always looked effortless on the family table.\n\nNevertheless, families who persist often describe the process as more rewarding than they expected. Beyond the recipe itself, conversations held in the kitchen frequently drift into older family stories, names of relatives no longer living, and small details about <b>everyday</b> life decades earlier that would otherwise go unmentioned. In this way, a single recorded recipe can preserve far more history than its ingredient list alone suggests.\n\nFood historians note that this quiet effort mirrors a wider cultural trend: as families spread further apart and traditional gatherings grow rarer, people increasingly search for small, tangible ways to stay connected to relatives and the past. A handwritten recipe card, imperfect and incomplete as it may be, often becomes a treasured object passed down long after the dish itself has been forgotten by everyone except the one page where it survives."
