        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "Psychologists have long studied the bond between parents and children, but a growing body of research is turning its attention to a different pair: grandparents and grandchildren. Activities as simple as gardening, baking or playing cards together, researchers find, can produce benefits that are far from <b>negligible</b> for both generations involved.\n\nFor grandchildren, time spent with grandparents often provides a different kind of attention from that given by busy parents. Grandparents, typically less rushed by work deadlines, can afford to let a child take twenty minutes to plant a single seed without hurrying the process along. <u>This unhurried attention appears to help children develop patience and a sense of accomplishment that more structured, fast-paced activities rarely provide.</u> Many grandchildren also report learning practical skills, from cooking to simple repairs, that they rarely encounter elsewhere.\n\n[I] The benefits, however, flow in both directions. [II] Grandparents who regularly spend time with grandchildren report lower levels of loneliness and a stronger sense of purpose. [III] Teaching a skill, however small, gives older adults a meaningful role beyond retirement. [IV] Some studies even associate frequent contact with grandchildren with better cognitive health in later life.\n\nNone of this requires elaborate planning or expensive outings. A shared recipe, a half-finished jigsaw puzzle, or a few rows of carrots can be enough to create a lasting connection. Family counsellors increasingly recommend that parents actively encourage such ordinary, unstructured time between the generations, rather than reserving grandparent visits for special occasions alone. The quiet, repeated moments, <b>they</b> argue, tend to matter more in the long run than any single grand celebration."
