        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "Across many neighbourhoods, small plots of unused land are being transformed into shared vegetable gardens where families grow their own food side by side. What began largely as a way to cut grocery costs has, in many communities, become something far more <b>significant</b>: a space where generations mix, skills are exchanged, and neighbours become friends.\n\nFor many participants, the appeal goes well beyond fresh produce. <u>Working the soil together gives neighbours who might never otherwise speak to each other a natural, unforced reason for regular conversation.</u> A shared watering schedule or a sudden pest problem becomes an easy opening for small talk that, over months, can deepen into genuine friendship.\n\nChildren, too, benefit in ways that reach beyond the garden gate. Those who help grow their own vegetables are, according to several studies, more willing to try unfamiliar foods and better able to explain where their meals actually come from. Older residents, meanwhile, often take on an informal teaching role, sharing techniques <b>passed</b> down from their own parents and grandparents.\n\nNone of this happens automatically, of course. Community gardens require a degree of organisation – clear rules about plot boundaries, shared tools and watering turns – to avoid the very conflicts they are meant to prevent. Most successful gardens appoint a small committee of volunteers to settle disputes and coordinate tasks. With reasonable management in place, however, a patch of once-empty land can grow considerably more than vegetables: <b>it</b> can grow a genuine sense of community."
