        "passage": "Every year, millions of tonnes of plastic end up in the world's oceans. Bottles, bags and fishing nets drift for years, slowly breaking into tiny pieces known as microplastics. Because plastic does not rot away like food or paper, it can <b>persist</b> in the sea for hundreds of years.\n\nMarine animals suffer most. Turtles mistake floating bags for jellyfish, and seabirds feed plastic fragments to their chicks. <u>When animals swallow plastic, they may feel full without receiving any nourishment, and many slowly starve.</u> Scientists have also found microplastics in fish, and there is growing concern about what this means for people who eat seafood.\n\n[I] Solving the problem needs action at every level. [II] Some countries have banned single-use plastic bags, and several cities have introduced deposit schemes for bottles. [III] Companies are developing packaging made from materials that break down more quickly. [IV] Ordinary people, meanwhile, can refuse unnecessary plastic and take part in beach clean-ups.\n\nNevertheless, clean-ups alone cannot solve the problem. Unless the amount of plastic produced is reduced, the oceans will continue to fill up faster than volunteers can clear them. Experts therefore stress that prevention is far more effective than cure. <b>It</b> begins at home, with the simple decision to use less."
