        "passage": "As cities grow, green space is shrinking. Parks are replaced by apartment blocks, and children who once played on grass now spend their free time indoors. Yet research suggests that people living near trees and gardens are healthier and happier than <b>those</b> who live in concrete surroundings.\n\nThe benefits are not only personal. Trees absorb dust and carbon dioxide, provide shade and keep cities cooler in summer. <u>Shade from a mature tree can make the air around it noticeably cooler, saving energy that would otherwise be used for air conditioning.</u> Green areas also absorb heavy rain, which reduces the risk of flooding in the streets.\n\nCities are finding creative ways to add greenery. Some have planted trees along busy roads, while others have turned unused rooftops and walls into gardens. In crowded areas where land is <b>scarce</b>, even small pocket parks and community gardens make a difference. Residents often take part in planting and caring for these spaces, which strengthens the sense of community.\n\nOf course, green spaces need <b>maintenance</b>. Plants must be watered, rubbish must be cleared and paths must be repaired. Without regular care, a park can quickly become neglected and unsafe. City governments therefore need to plan not only how to build green spaces but also how to look after them for years to come."
