        "passage": "For many years, Viet Nam relied mainly on hydropower and coal to produce electricity. As the economy grew and demand for power increased, however, the country began to look for cleaner sources. Solar and wind farms now supply a growing share of electricity, and the government has set <b>ambitious</b> targets for increasing their use.\n\nThe advantages of renewable energy are clear. It produces little or no pollution, and its fuel, sunshine and wind, is free and will never run out. <u>Moreover, small solar systems can bring electricity to remote villages where it would be too expensive to build power lines.</u> Installing solar panels on roofs also allows households and factories to produce their own power and to reduce their bills.\n\n[I] Renewable energy nevertheless poses some difficulties. [II] Sunshine and wind are not constant, so power stations must store electricity or use other sources when the weather is unfavourable. [III] Large solar and wind farms need a lot of land, which may compete with farming or forests. [IV] In addition, the electricity grid must be upgraded so that it can carry power from distant places to cities.\n\nExperts believe that the future will depend on a mixture of sources. Renewable energy will supply more and more power, while efficiency measures reduce the amount that we need. Households can help by choosing efficient appliances and by turning off devices that are not in use. Countries that invest early in clean energy will probably enjoy lower costs and cleaner air than <b>those</b> that wait."
