        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "In many parts of the world, fresh water is becoming scarcer. Growing cities, farming and industry use more of it every year, while droughts make supplies less reliable. One simple response is rainwater harvesting: collecting rain from roofs and storing it in tanks for later use.\n\nIn rural areas of Viet Nam, families have long kept large jars to catch rain during the wet season. Modern systems are more sophisticated, with gutters, filters and underground tanks, but the principle is the same. Households that collect rainwater usually spend less on their water bills than <b>those</b> that depend completely on the public supply. <u>Rainwater can be used for watering gardens, washing and flushing toilets, and after proper treatment it can also be drunk.</u>\n\nNevertheless, there are <b>limitations</b>. Rain does not fall evenly through the year, so tanks may be empty in the dry season. Roofs can collect dust, leaves and bird droppings, which must be filtered out. Tanks also need regular cleaning to prevent mosquitoes from breeding in them. Without maintenance, a rainwater system may do more harm than good.\n\nCities can encourage rainwater harvesting by offering lower taxes or grants to households that install tanks, and by including them in the design of new buildings. Schools, too, can set an example; a school that collects rain from its large roofs can water its gardens and teach students about <b>conservation</b> at the same time."
