        "passage": "Supermarkets have long relied on plastic to keep fruit and vegetables looking fresh, wrapping cucumbers, peppers and apples in a thin film that most shoppers barely notice. In recent years, however, a small but growing number of stores have begun removing this packaging altogether, arguing that the convenience it offers is far from <b>justifiable</b> given its environmental cost.\n\n<u>Early results from stores that have removed plastic wrapping suggest that most customers adapt quickly, with complaints dropping sharply within the first few weeks of the change.</u> Store managers report that shoppers who initially worried about hygiene or freshness generally come to accept loose produce once they see that it sells and spoils no differently from wrapped alternatives.\n\nNot every product, however, can be so easily unwrapped. Soft fruits such as berries bruise easily and spoil faster without protection, meaning that some packaging continues to serve a genuine, <b>practical</b> purpose rather than existing purely out of habit. Retailers experimenting with alternatives, such as reusable containers or biodegradable nets, say that finding a suitable replacement for every product remains a slow, case-by-case process.\n\nStill, the overall trend is encouraging. Several large retail chains have committed to removing a fixed percentage of plastic packaging within the next few years, and smaller shops are increasingly following their lead. Industry analysts suggest that pressure from environmentally conscious customers, rather than government regulation alone, has been the single most powerful force driving this change so far."
