        "passage": "City planners have traditionally viewed trees as a pleasant finishing touch, added once the more pressing matters of roads, housing and drainage were settled. Recent research, however, suggests that this view badly <b>underestimates</b> what urban trees actually contribute to a functioning city.\n\nBeyond their obvious aesthetic appeal, trees perform a surprising range of practical services. <u>A single large tree can absorb enough rainwater during a storm to meaningfully reduce the risk of flooding on the street beneath it, while also filtering a measurable amount of air pollution from passing traffic.</u> In hot weather, the shade a mature tree provides can lower surrounding temperatures by several degrees, easing pressure on both public health and electricity grids strained by air conditioning.\n\n[I] Despite these benefits, urban tree cover continues to shrink in many fast-growing cities. [II] Construction projects frequently remove mature trees that took decades to grow, replacing them, if at all, with much younger saplings. [III] Budget constraints often mean that ongoing tree maintenance is one of the first services to be cut when municipal funds run short. [IV] The result, researchers warn, is a slow but steady loss of a resource that is remarkably difficult and expensive to rebuild once gone.\n\nSome cities have begun treating tree cover differently, writing it into formal planning requirements rather than treating it as an optional extra. Planners in these cities report that developers, once required to account for tree loss, increasingly find ways to preserve existing trees or commit to <b>substantial</b> replacement planting, suggesting that stronger rules, not simply good intentions, may be what finally reverses the decline."
