        "passage": "In the centre of a city once known mainly for its heavy traffic and grey concrete, a stretch of abandoned railway line has quietly become one of its most celebrated green spaces. What began as an informal gathering spot for a handful of local gardeners has, over the past decade, grown into a <b>celebrated</b> linear park stretching for several kilometres above the streets below.\n\n<u>Local residents, initially sceptical that anything could be done with a crumbling, disused structure, gradually became its most enthusiastic defenders once the first wildflowers began to bloom along its length.</u> What had been considered an eyesore and a safety hazard was, within a few years, attracting visitors from across the city and featuring in guidebooks as a must-see attraction.\n\nThe transformation was not without its critics. Some long-time residents worried that the park's growing popularity would drive up rents in the surrounding neighbourhood, pushing out the very communities who had first imagined it. Urban planners acknowledge this tension as a genuine and <b>persistent</b> challenge, one that has emerged in nearly every city that has pursued a similar project.\n\nDespite these concerns, the park is now widely regarded as a model for other cities considering what to do with their own abandoned infrastructure. Officials visiting from elsewhere frequently ask the same question: how much did it cost, and could something similar work at home? The honest answer, organisers say, is that the structure mattered less than the years of patient community involvement that turned a discarded railway into a shared, living space."
