        "passage": "Fresh water often feels inexhaustible to anyone who has never experienced a serious shortage. Turning on a tap and watching clean water flow instantly seems so ordinary that few people stop to consider how <b>finite</b> the supply actually is, or how unevenly it is distributed across the planet.\n\nThe arithmetic behind the problem is sobering. Although roughly seventy per cent of the Earth's surface is covered in water, less than three per cent of it is fresh, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps and deep underground reserves. <u>What remains readily accessible for drinking, farming and industry amounts to a surprisingly small share of the planet's total water, and that share is not growing even as the number of people relying on it continues to rise.</u>\n\n[I] Agriculture alone consumes a staggering proportion of the water that is available. [II] Growing the food needed for a single meal can require hundreds, sometimes thousands, of litres of water once every stage of production is accounted for. [III] Industry and household use, though significant, together account for considerably less than farming does worldwide. [IV] This imbalance means that even modest improvements in agricultural water efficiency can have an outsized effect on overall supply.\n\nSolutions exist, but none is a single simple fix. Drip irrigation, which delivers water directly to plant roots rather than spraying it broadly, can reduce agricultural water use substantially without <b>sacrificing</b> crop yields. Household habits matter too, even if their individual impact is smaller: shorter showers, full washing-machine loads and prompt repairs to leaking pipes collectively reduce demand on a system that is under far more strain than most users ever realise."
