        "passage": "Every weekday morning, the streets surrounding thousands of schools around the world briefly transform into some of the most congested, polluted spaces in the entire city. Traffic engineers describe this phenomenon, somewhat <b>bluntly</b>, as the school run problem, and increasingly argue that it deserves far more attention than it typically receives.\n\nThe scale of the issue is easy to underestimate. <u>A significant proportion of these car journeys cover distances that most adults would consider entirely walkable, meaning that much of the resulting congestion and pollution is, in a sense, avoidable rather than necessary.</u> Short trips are also disproportionately polluting, since car engines operate least efficiently in the first few minutes after starting, before they have warmed to an ideal temperature.\n\n[I] Persuading families to change their habits, however, is rarely straightforward. [II] Many parents cite safety concerns, pointing to the very traffic their own driving contributes to as the reason walking feels too dangerous. [III] Time pressure plays a role too, since driving often fits more easily into a hurried morning schedule than walking does. [IV] Weather and distance present genuine obstacles for some families, even when the will to change exists.\n\nSchools that have successfully reduced car journeys rarely rely on a single solution. Organised 'walking buses,' in which an adult volunteer walks a fixed route collecting children along the way, directly address safety concerns while removing the need for every parent to walk individually. Combined with secure cycle storage and clearly marked safe routes, such <b>modest</b> measures have, in several documented cases, cut school-gate traffic by a noticeable margin within a single term."
