        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "In a warehouse on the outskirts of a mid-sized city, workers in protective gear carefully take apart discarded laptops and phones piece by piece, separating circuit boards, batteries and casings into neatly labelled bins. What looks, at first glance, like an unusually meticulous form of rubbish sorting is, in fact, a small but <b>profitable</b> business built entirely on materials most people consider worthless.\n\n<u>Every bin in the warehouse represents a material that, extracted carefully and in sufficient quantity, can be sold back into the supply chains that originally produced the devices in the first place.</u> Gold and copper recovered from old circuit boards, for instance, are refined and sold to manufacturers who would otherwise have to mine the same metals from the ground.\n\nThe business model depends entirely on steady supply, which is precisely where the greatest challenge lies. Despite handling thousands of devices each month, the warehouse's manager admits that the operation reaches only a small fraction of the e-waste generated in the surrounding region, with the <b>remainder</b> still ending up in drawers, landfills or informal, unregulated scrapyards.\n\nExpanding operations like this one, industry analysts argue, depends less on improving the recycling technology itself, which is already reasonably efficient, and more on building the collection networks needed to actually get discarded devices through the warehouse door in the first place. Without that final link, even the most advanced recycling facility can only process what it manages to receive."
