        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "Roughly a third of all food produced worldwide is never eaten, and a significant share of that waste ends up buried in landfill sites rather than returned to the soil it could usefully enrich. Composting, once dismissed as a <b>fringe</b> hobby for keen gardeners, is increasingly promoted as a practical solution available to almost any household.\n\nThe basic process is simpler than many people assume. <u>Fruit peels, vegetable scraps and coffee grounds, left to break down in a bin with the right balance of air and moisture, gradually transform into a dark, crumbly material that gardeners prize for its ability to improve soil quality.</u> No special equipment is strictly necessary, though a simple bin helps keep the process tidy and discourages pests.\n\n[I] Landfill disposal, by contrast, treats food waste very differently. [II] Buried under layers of other rubbish, organic waste decomposes without access to oxygen, a process that releases methane, a gas far more damaging to the climate than carbon dioxide. [III] A single landfill can emit significant quantities of this gas for decades after it has officially closed. [IV] Composting avoids this problem almost entirely, since the aerobic process it relies on produces comparatively little methane.\n\nDespite these clear advantages, composting remains a minority practice in many places, often due to simple unfamiliarity rather than any real difficulty. Community workshops, like those increasingly run by schools and local councils, appear to be remarkably effective at closing this knowledge gap, turning an <b>intimidating</b>-sounding process into something approachable enough for an absolute beginner to try within a single afternoon."
