        "answer": 0,
        "passage": "On the roof of an apartment building in a densely packed city district, a row of neatly organised compost bins has quietly become one of the neighbourhood's more unusual meeting points. What started as one resident's attempt to deal with her own food waste has, over three years, grown into a shared facility used by nearly every household in the building, <b>managed</b> entirely by volunteers on a rotating weekly schedule.\n\n<u>Residents who once threw away their fruit peels and vegetable scraps without a second thought now routinely set them aside for the rooftop bins, often remarking that the habit took far less adjustment than they had originally expected.</u> The compost produced is shared among residents who keep balcony gardens, with any surplus donated to a nearby community garden that supplies vegetables to a local food bank.\n\nMaintaining the system has not been entirely without friction. A handful of residents initially worried about smells or pests, concerns the volunteer organisers addressed by introducing covered bins and a strict rule against adding meat or dairy scraps. One early sceptic, now among the system's most <b>enthusiastic</b> users, admits she assumed the whole project would quietly fail within a few months.\n\nWhat keeps the rooftop compost system running, according to the volunteers who maintain it, is less any particular technique than the simple fact that enough residents have come to see their food scraps as a shared resource rather than rubbish to be discarded. Scaling the idea to larger buildings, they suspect, depends mainly on finding a handful of residents willing to start."
