        "passage": "Despite decades of public information campaigns, a surprisingly large share of household recycling never actually gets recycled, contaminated by food residue, mixed with non-recyclable materials, or simply placed in the wrong bin altogether. Community recycling drives, once seen as a <b>quaint</b> local tradition, are increasingly recognised as a practical way to close this persistent gap.\n\nThe appeal of these drives lies in their directness. <u>Rather than relying on residents to correctly interpret printed sorting guides on their own, volunteers stand ready to answer questions, demonstrate proper sorting technique, and gently correct mistakes before they become habits.</u> For many households, this face-to-face guidance proves far more effective than any leaflet ever could.\n\n[I] Contamination remains one of the biggest obstacles to effective recycling worldwide. [II] A single greasy pizza box or a bag of mixed materials can render an entire batch of otherwise recyclable material unusable. [III] Facilities that receive heavily contaminated loads often have no choice but to send the whole batch to landfill. [IV] Recycling drives, by catching these mistakes at the source, help prevent this costly outcome.\n\nCritics sometimes argue that such drives are too small in scale to matter, given how little material any single event collects. Organisers counter that the real value lies not in the volume collected but in the habits formed, as residents who learn to sort correctly at a drive tend to carry that knowledge home, improving the quality of their household recycling for years afterward, long after the <b>novelty</b> of the event itself has faded."
