        "passage": "On a row of streets in a quiet residential district, a cluster of brightly painted recycling collection points has quietly become a small point of neighbourhood pride. What started as one street's attempt to reduce overflowing bins has, over two years, grown into a network used by residents across the entire district, <b>coordinated</b> entirely by volunteers on a rotating weekly schedule.\n\n<u>Residents who once left bottles and cans scattered among their general rubbish now routinely set them aside for the collection points, often remarking that the change required far less effort than they had originally feared.</u> The materials collected are sold to a regional recycling cooperative, with the proceeds used to fund small neighbourhood improvements such as new benches and tree planting.\n\nMaintaining the network has not been entirely without friction. A handful of residents were initially <b>reluctant</b>, worried that the bins would attract litter or look untidy on their street. Volunteer organisers addressed this by introducing covered, clearly labelled bins and a simple schedule for regular collection. One early sceptic, now among the network's most dedicated volunteers, admits she assumed the whole project would quietly fail within a few months.\n\nWhat keeps the recycling network running, according to the volunteers who maintain it, is less any particular technique than the simple fact that enough residents have come to see their recyclables as a shared resource rather than rubbish to be discarded. Scaling the idea to neighbouring districts, they suspect, depends mainly on finding a handful of residents willing to start."
