        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Music festivals attract millions of visitors around the world every year. Some last a single evening, while others continue for a week or more, and they range from small local events to huge gatherings with hundreds of performers. Whatever their size, festivals offer an <b>atmosphere</b> that is difficult to find at an ordinary concert: crowds sing together, strangers dance side by side and performers share their enjoyment with the audience.\n\nFestivals also benefit local communities. Visitors spend money in hotels, restaurants and shops, and organisers often employ local people as guides, cooks and technicians. <u>For a small town, a successful festival can bring more income in a few days than it would normally earn in several months.</u> Many festivals also give young musicians a chance to perform before a large audience, which may launch their careers.\n\n[I] Yet festivals can have disadvantages. [II] Large crowds produce noise, traffic jams and a great deal of rubbish. [III] Residents of nearby neighbourhoods may find it hard to sleep or to move around. [IV] Some festivals have therefore introduced rules to reduce their impact, such as recycling stations, quiet hours and shuttle buses.\n\nResponsible organisers also look for ways to share the benefits. Some offer reduced-price tickets to local residents, and others donate a part of the profits to community projects. Visitors can help, too, by using public transport, taking their rubbish home and respecting the neighbourhood. When festival-goers behave considerately, <b>they</b> help to ensure that the event is welcome for many years to come."
