        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "In an age when almost any song can be streamed instantly on a phone, the simple act of singing together in a group might seem like a <b>relic</b> of an earlier era. Yet community choirs, far from disappearing, are reportedly attracting more members than at any point in recent decades.\n\nResearchers studying group singing point to effects that recorded music cannot replicate. <u>Singing in time with other people, breathing together and listening closely to blend one's voice with the group, appears to produce a sense of connection that listening alone simply does not create.</u> Several studies have linked regular choir participation to lower reported stress and a stronger sense of belonging.\n\n[I] Choirs also offer something increasingly rare: a reason to gather regularly with the same group of people. [II] In communities where social ties have weakened, this regular contact can matter as much as the music itself. [III] Members frequently describe their choir as a kind of extended family, rather than simply a hobby. [IV] Few other activities bring together such a wide range of ages and backgrounds on equal terms.\n\nNone of this suggests that streaming music is somehow harmful, only that it serves a different purpose. Listening alone can be effortless and endlessly convenient, but it rarely produces the sense of shared achievement that choir members describe after a successful performance, a feeling that keeps many of them returning week after week long after the initial <b>novelty</b> has worn off."
