        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "The appeal of open mic nights has grown steadily in towns and cities where few other low-pressure stages exist for new performers to practise in front of a real audience. What began decades ago in small cafes and bars has since spread to schools, community centres and even libraries, with organisers reporting that demand for performance slots consistently outpaces the time available.\n\nPart of the appeal lies in the format's low stakes. <u>Unlike a formal concert, where a single mistake can feel like a public failure, an open mic night treats imperfection as an ordinary, almost expected, part of the evening.</u> Performers are rarely judged harshly for forgetting lyrics or fumbling a chord, since most of the audience has either performed themselves or plans to eventually.\n\nThis forgiving atmosphere has made open mic nights a particularly <b>valuable</b> training ground for young musicians who might otherwise avoid performing altogether. [I] Regularly stepping onto a small stage, even an unglamorous one in a school hall, appears to build confidence that eventually carries over into larger performances. [II] Several professional musicians have credited early open mic experiences as a turning point in their careers. [III] Teachers who organise these events often note a noticeable change in shy students after just a few appearances. [IV] Few other school activities offer such a direct and repeatable way to practise performing in front of others.\n\nCritics occasionally dismiss open mic nights as unpolished or amateurish, missing what organisers see as the entire point. The goal is not a flawless show but a <b>accessible</b> space where anyone willing to try can gradually improve, one short performance at a time."
