        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "A teenager in a small rural town with no music shop nearby taught herself to play guitar almost entirely through free video tutorials, practising in her bedroom for an hour most evenings after finishing her homework. What started as a way to fill quiet evenings gradually became a serious pursuit, as she worked her way through increasingly <b>advanced</b> lessons posted by musicians she had never met.\n\n<u>Without a teacher to correct her technique in person, she relied on filming herself and comparing the footage carefully against the tutorial videos, slowly catching and fixing small mistakes on her own.</u> After nearly two years of this routine, she posted a short video of her own playing online mostly out of curiosity, never expecting anyone beyond her family to watch it.\n\nThe video was shared far more widely than she anticipated, eventually reaching several thousand viewers and a handful of encouraging comments from musicians she had long admired. She remains <b>modest</b> about the attention, insisting that her technique is still far from perfect and that she has simply been consistent rather than naturally gifted.\n\nWhat she wants other self-taught beginners to understand, she says, is that progress from video tutorials is almost always slower and less visible than it looks from the outside, and that the only real secret is returning to practise again the next evening, even when no improvement seems obvious yet."
