        "passage": "Playing a solo piece well is widely admired, but orchestral musicians often argue that the far harder skill, and the one audiences rarely notice, is learning to listen more than to play. A trained soloist can sound impressive within months, whereas an orchestra member typically needs years to develop the constant <b>awareness</b> that ensemble playing demands.\n\nThis awareness involves far more than simply staying in time with a conductor's beat. <u>Each musician must continuously adjust their own volume, tone and timing in response to dozens of other musicians playing simultaneously, often without consciously thinking about the adjustment at all.</u> Experienced players describe this as developing a kind of peripheral hearing, a sense of the whole sound rather than just their own part.\n\n[I] Young musicians joining an orchestra for the first time often find this adjustment genuinely difficult. [II] Having practised largely alone at home, they are unused to constantly listening outward rather than inward. [III] Conductors report that this skill, once developed, rarely fades even if a musician later stops playing regularly. [IV] Many former orchestra members say they still instinctively notice musical balance decades after leaving school.\n\nFor this reason, educators increasingly argue that orchestral experience deserves recognition as more than a musical activity. The <b>disciplined</b> listening it demands, they suggest, builds habits of attention and cooperation that prove useful in settings that have nothing to do with music at all."
