        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "In a small retirement community with no history of hosting live music, a handful of residents who had once played instruments decades earlier decided, almost on a whim, to form an informal orchestra using whatever instruments they could find in their own homes. What began as a single afternoon of rusty, <b>tentative</b> playing gradually became a weekly gathering that now draws more than twenty participants.\n\n<u>Many of the musicians had not touched their instruments in thirty or forty years, and several admitted to feeling embarrassed by how much their playing had declined since their younger days.</u> Rather than discouraging anyone, this shared rustiness seemed to bring the group closer together, since nobody felt singled out as the weakest player.\n\nThe group's first public performance, a short set at the community's spring fair, was met with far more enthusiasm than any of the musicians had <b>anticipated</b>. Several audience members, themselves former musicians, approached afterward asking how they might join. A volunteer coordinator who helped organise the group recalls assuming interest would fade within a few months, much like other informal hobby groups often do.\n\nWhat has kept the orchestra together, according to its members, is less the quality of the performances than the simple pleasure of making music together again after years away from it. Other retirement communities considering something similar, they suggest, should not worry about sounding polished and should instead focus on simply getting instruments out of their cases."
