        "passage": "Battle of the bands competitions have existed in roughly their current form for decades, yet their popularity among school and community groups shows little sign of fading, even as streaming has transformed how most people listen to music. Part of their <b>enduring</b> appeal seems to lie in what they offer that recorded music cannot: a single, unrepeatable live performance judged in real time.\n\nUnlike a recording, which can be endlessly revised until it sounds perfect, a competition performance must succeed or fail in the moment. <u>This pressure, rather than discouraging young musicians, appears to push many bands toward a level of rehearsal and cohesion they might never otherwise have attempted.</u> Band members frequently describe the weeks before a competition as some of the most intensive practice of their musical lives.\n\n[I] Judges at these events typically score performances on a combination of technical skill, stage presence and originality. [II] This broad scoring system means a technically modest band with strong stage presence can sometimes outscore a more skilled but stiffer rival. [III] Several former competitors have credited this balance with teaching them that performance is about more than just playing the right notes. [IV] Many say this lesson shaped how they approached music long after the competition itself was forgotten.\n\nCritics sometimes argue that competitive formats encourage unhealthy rivalry among young musicians. Organisers generally counter that most participants rate the shared experience of rehearsing and performing together as more <b>memorable</b> than whether their band actually won."
