        "passage": "Industry figures tracking music sales have reported a surprising trend over the past decade: vinyl records, a format many assumed had been permanently retired by the arrival of streaming, have steadily increased in sales each year, often outselling compact discs entirely. Much of this growth, researchers note, comes not from older collectors returning to a familiar format but from younger listeners trying vinyl for the first time.\n\nWhat draws these newcomers in is not always practicality. <u>Streaming remains far cheaper and more convenient than buying records, yet many younger buyers describe something about the physical ritual of playing a record, placing it on a turntable and watching it spin, that a phone screen simply cannot offer.</u> Large cover artwork, liner notes and the faint crackle of analogue sound are frequently mentioned as part of the appeal.\n\n[I] Some industry analysts remain <b>sceptical</b> that this renewed interest will continue indefinitely. [II] They point out that vinyl sales, while growing, still represent a tiny fraction of total music consumption worldwide. [III] Record shop owners, however, report steady demand from customers who treat buying a record as a deliberate, occasional purchase rather than a daily habit. [IV] This pattern suggests vinyl may be settling into a smaller but stable niche rather than disappearing again.\n\nWhatever the format's long-term future, collectors themselves seem largely unconcerned with industry predictions. For many, the appeal lies simply in owning something tangible in an age when most music exists only as data, a feeling that streaming, however <b>convenient</b>, has yet to replicate."
