        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "In a small town where most shops along the main street have closed over the past decade, one secondhand record shop has somehow managed not only to survive but to steadily expand its customer base. The shop's owner, a man in his sixties who has run the business since his twenties, attributes this unlikely success to a <b>stubborn</b> refusal to modernise the shop's appearance or atmosphere.\n\n<u>Rather than installing bright lighting or a modern point-of-sale system, he has kept the shop almost exactly as it looked decades ago, crammed with crates of records organised in a system only he fully understands.</u> Younger customers, he has noticed, often seem drawn precisely to this lack of polish, treating a visit to the shop as something closer to an adventure than an ordinary purchase.\n\nNot every change has been resisted, however. The owner remains notably <b>flexible</b> about accepting records in any condition, offering trade-in credit even for albums with visible scratches or damaged covers, a policy some rival shops consider overly generous. He insists this openness is precisely what keeps new collectors returning, since nobody feels turned away for owning an imperfect copy.\n\nWhat has kept the shop going, he suggests, is less any clever business strategy than a simple refusal to treat the shop as merely a transaction, choosing instead to let every visitor browse for as long as they like without ever feeling rushed toward the till."
