        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "Among the many claims made about music's effect on the mind, few are as well supported by research as its influence on memory, particularly in older adults experiencing memory loss. Caregivers working with patients who struggle to recall recent events or familiar faces have long reported something striking: songs learned decades earlier often remain <b>accessible</b> long after other memories have faded.\n\nResearchers studying this phenomenon suggest that musical memory may be stored somewhat differently from other types of memory in the brain. <u>A familiar melody, once heard many times across a person's life, appears to activate regions of the brain that remain relatively well preserved even as other areas are affected by memory loss.</u> This may explain why a patient unable to recognise a family member can still sing every word of a song from their youth.\n\n[I] Care facilities have increasingly begun incorporating personalised playlists into daily routines based on this research. [II] Rather than playing generic background music, staff work with families to compile songs tied to specific meaningful periods in a patient's life. [III] Patients exposed to these personalised playlists often show noticeable, if temporary, improvements in mood and alertness. [IV] Some caregivers describe these moments as among the clearest signs of connection they witness during an otherwise difficult day.\n\nNone of this suggests music can reverse memory loss itself, and researchers are careful to avoid overstating the evidence. What it does suggest, more modestly, is that a familiar song can sometimes reach a person in ways that conversation alone no longer can, offering moments of <b>clarity</b> that families often treasure precisely because they are so rare."
