        "passage": "At a retirement home that had previously offered few structured activities beyond occasional card games, a newly hired activities coordinator introduced a weekly music programme almost as an experiment, uncertain whether residents would show much interest. The response, she recalls, was far more <b>enthusiastic</b> than she had anticipated, with attendance growing steadily within the first few months.\n\n<u>Rather than simply playing recorded music in the background, she asked each resident individually about songs that mattered to them, then built sessions around these personal requests rather than a generic playlist.</u> Residents who rarely spoke during other activities, staff noticed, often became noticeably more animated once a personally meaningful song began to play.\n\nNot every session has gone smoothly. Some residents initially seemed <b>indifferent</b>, showing little visible reaction regardless of which songs were chosen, which the coordinator admits was occasionally discouraging. Over time, however, even some of these residents began quietly humming along, suggesting the programme's effects were not always immediately obvious.\n\nWhat the coordinator hopes other care facilities will take from her experience is that building a music programme around personal history, rather than convenience, appears to matter far more than most staff initially assume, even when the benefits take weeks rather than days to become visible."
