        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "In crowded cities, many residents have little contact with their neighbours. People leave early for work, return late and spend their evenings indoors. As a result, communities that were once close can become isolated, and loneliness grows, especially among elderly people.\n\nCommunity gardens offer one answer to this problem. In these shared spaces, neighbours grow vegetables and flowers together on unused land. Gardeners who work side by side soon begin to chat, share advice and exchange seeds, and friendships often develop. Neighbours who garden together tend to feel more attached to their area than <b>those</b> who rarely meet. <u>Many participants say that the garden has given them not only fresh food but also a reason to meet people they would otherwise never have spoken to.</u>\n\nCommunity gardens also benefit the environment. Plants absorb carbon dioxide, and growing food locally reduces the need to transport vegetables over long distances. Compost made from kitchen waste enriches the soil, so less rubbish is sent to landfill. Even a small plot can become a home for bees and butterflies.\n\nGardens do face difficulties. Land in cities is expensive, and owners may be <b>reluctant</b> to allow its use for free. Water and tools must also be provided, and someone has to look after the garden during holidays. Local councils can help by offering unused plots, while schools and companies can donate equipment. With such support, even a small garden can <b>flourish</b> and bring a neighbourhood together."
