        "passage": "Viet Nam, like many countries, has a growing number of elderly people. Many live with their children, but a significant minority live alone, especially in rural areas where younger family members have moved to cities for work. For these older people, loneliness can be as <b>serious</b> a problem as poor health or low income.\n\nCommunities can respond in practical ways. Volunteers can visit older neighbours, help with shopping and cooking, or simply sit and talk. <u>Even a short weekly visit can reduce feelings of isolation and give elderly people something to look forward to.</u> Some neighbourhoods have set up clubs where older residents meet for exercise, singing and games.\n\n[I] Young people can play a special part. [II] They can teach elderly relatives how to use smartphones to call their children and grandchildren. [III] In return, older people often share stories, skills and wisdom that cannot be found in books. [IV] Such exchanges build understanding between generations.\n\nSchools and local authorities can support these efforts. Students can earn service credits for regular visits, and councils can provide transport so that those who live far from community centres can still attend activities. Above all, society should treat elderly people as valued members rather than as a burden. When communities respect their older residents, <b>they</b> create an environment in which everybody can look forward to growing old with dignity."
