        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "Hospitals rely on a steady, uninterrupted supply of donated blood, yet maintaining this supply remains surprisingly difficult, since blood cannot be manufactured artificially and can only be stored safely for a limited period. School and community donation drives, once considered a relatively <b>minor</b> contribution to overall supply, have become an increasingly important source as other donation channels have struggled to keep pace with demand.\n\nWhat makes school drives particularly valuable, according to hospital staff, is not simply the number of donations collected but the reliability of their timing. <u>Because school terms follow a predictable calendar, hospitals can plan around these events with a confidence that donations from the general public, which tend to fluctuate unpredictably, rarely allow.</u> A single well-organised school event can sometimes supply a noticeable share of a hospital's weekly requirement.\n\n[I] Nervousness remains the most commonly cited reason students give for avoiding their first donation. [II] Nurses who run these events say most of this nervousness fades within moments of actually sitting down to donate. [III] Many first-time donors report feeling surprised at how quick and manageable the process turns out to be. [IV] This gap between expectation and experience is, according to staff, the main obstacle standing between a hesitant student and a completed donation.\n\nOrganisers of these events generally avoid pressuring any student to donate against their wishes, emphasising instead that volunteering to assist on the day is just as valuable a contribution. What matters most, they suggest, is building a <b>consistent</b> culture of participation that outlasts any single student's time at the school."
