        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "Loneliness among elderly residents in care homes has drawn growing attention from researchers, many of whom describe its effects on health as comparable to more widely recognised risk factors. Programmes pairing student volunteers with individual residents, once viewed as a relatively <b>casual</b> community service activity, are increasingly being studied for their measurable effects on resident wellbeing.\n\nWhat distinguishes effective programmes from less successful ones, according to coordinators who run them, is less the frequency of visits than their consistency. <u>A resident who can reliably expect the same volunteer every fortnight appears to benefit far more than one who receives occasional, unpredictable visits from a rotating group of strangers.</u> This consistency, coordinators suggest, is what transforms a visit from a pleasant interruption into something closer to an actual relationship.\n\n[I] Student volunteers often arrive expecting the main challenge to be finding things to discuss. [II] Most report that this concern fades almost immediately once a resident begins sharing stories from their own life. [III] Several volunteers describe becoming genuinely invested in hearing updates about a resident's family or health between visits. [IV] This shift from obligation to genuine interest is, according to coordinators, the clearest sign that a pairing is working well.\n\nCritics occasionally question whether short, scheduled visits can meaningfully address a problem as serious as chronic loneliness. Coordinators generally agree the programmes are not a complete solution, but argue that a reliable, <b>recurring</b> connection matters considerably more to an isolated resident than its modest scale might suggest."
