        "answer": 0,
        "passage": "Community clean-up days have become a familiar fixture in many neighbourhoods, yet organisers who run them repeatedly report a pattern that surprises newer volunteers each time: the amount of litter uncovered rarely matches what a quick glance at the street would suggest. A road that appears reasonably tidy from a passing car often turns out, on closer inspection, to be <b>concealing</b> considerable rubbish in drains, behind bins and along narrow gaps between buildings.\n\nThis gap between appearance and reality shapes how experienced organisers train new volunteers. <u>Rather than simply instructing teams to walk the length of an assigned street, coordinators now emphasise checking specific hidden areas systematically, since skipping them can leave a street looking clean while still harbouring a significant amount of waste.</u> Teams that learn to check these areas typically collect noticeably more rubbish than those who do not.\n\n[I] This discovery initially unsettles many first-time volunteers who expected a straightforward, quick task. [II] Several describe feeling genuinely surprised at how much litter had gone unnoticed on streets they walked past regularly. [III] This surprise, coordinators note, often becomes one of the most commonly mentioned takeaways from the entire event. [IV] Many volunteers say it permanently changes how closely they notice litter afterward, even outside organised clean-up events.\n\nWhatever the psychological effect on volunteers, the practical benefit to the neighbourhood is more straightforward: streets cleaned thoroughly, rather than superficially, tend to accumulate new litter more slowly, since the hidden build-up that attracts further rubbish has genuinely been <b>eliminated</b> rather than simply hidden from view."
