        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Winter clothing donation drives run by schools and youth organisations face a persistent challenge that organisers say rarely gets discussed openly: a significant share of potential donors assume, often <b>mistakenly</b>, that only new or barely worn clothing is suitable to give away. This assumption, organisers report, quietly discourages many students from donating items that would otherwise have been perfectly usable.\n\nThe reality, according to volunteers who sort these donations each year, is considerably more forgiving than most donors assume. <u>A coat with a small mark or a slightly faded colour keeps a child just as warm as a brand-new one, and recipients in remote areas typically care far more about warmth and fit than about appearance.</u> Organisers actively try to correct this misconception each year, with mixed success.\n\n[I] Sorting volunteers report that donations increase noticeably once this message is communicated clearly beforehand. [II] Clear signage explaining that gently used, clean clothing is welcome appears to make a measurable difference to collection numbers. [III] Without this reassurance, many students reportedly leave perfectly good clothing at home, assuming it would not be considered acceptable. [IV] This gap between assumption and actual need represents, according to organisers, one of the largest untapped sources of potential donations each year.\n\nBeyond simply increasing the volume collected, correcting this misconception carries another benefit that organisers consider equally important: it <b>normalises</b> donating used items generally, a habit that may continue well beyond any single clothing drive."
