        "passage": "A teacher who had organised her school's winter clothes drive for several years without major changes decided one year, somewhat <b>reluctantly</b>, to try a new approach after noticing donation numbers had quietly declined over the previous two drives. Rather than simply repeating the usual announcement, she asked a small group of students to interview previous donors about why they had, or had not, contributed.\n\n<u>What the students discovered surprised the teacher considerably: a noticeable share of students who had not donated explained that they assumed only new clothing would be accepted, despite this never having been stated as a requirement.</u> None of the usual announcements had ever explicitly corrected this assumption.\n\nThe following year, student volunteers designed new signage and announcements specifically clarifying that clean, gently used clothing was not only accepted but genuinely preferred, since it was often more immediately wearable than stiff, unworn garments. Donations that year increased by a margin the teacher describes as <b>unexpected</b>, far exceeding what any previous change in collection dates or locations had ever achieved.\n\nShe now encourages other teachers running similar drives to ask directly why non-donors chose not to participate rather than assuming the reasons, suggesting that a simple, overlooked misunderstanding is often easier to fix than organisers initially expect."
