        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Extended hospital stays can be particularly difficult for young patients, whose days are often structured around treatment schedules that leave little room for anything resembling ordinary childhood activity. Volunteer storytelling programmes, introduced in many hospitals as a relatively <b>simple</b> way to fill some of this time, are increasingly credited by hospital staff with effects that extend beyond mere entertainment.\n\nNurses who work closely with young patients describe a noticeable shift in mood on days when storytelling volunteers visit. <u>Children who have spent the morning anxious about an upcoming procedure often become visibly calmer once a volunteer begins reading, suggesting the distraction offers something closer to genuine relief than simple entertainment.</u> Several nurses report that this calming effect occasionally makes subsequent procedures noticeably easier to carry out.\n\n[I] Volunteers themselves frequently arrive expecting their main task to be choosing age-appropriate books. [II] Many instead discover that their calm, steady presence matters just as much as the specific story chosen. [III] Coordinators note that even volunteers who initially feel nervous about reading aloud tend to settle quickly once a child begins responding to the story. [IV] This shift from nervous volunteer to confident reader often happens within a single session.\n\nHospital administrators considering whether to expand such programmes generally cite cost as the main obstacle, since coordinating volunteer schedules and background checks requires ongoing staff time. Advocates counter that the relatively <b>modest</b> administrative cost is easily justified by the calming effect reported by nurses and parents alike."
