        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "Schools responding to natural disasters often turn to small-scale fundraising fairs, in which individual classes run modest stalls selling food, crafts or simple games, rather than relying on a single large donation drive. Organisers describe this approach as deliberately <b>distributed</b>, spreading both the effort and the sense of ownership across as many students as possible rather than concentrating it in a few organisers.\n\nThis distributed structure appears to matter for reasons beyond simply raising money efficiently. <u>Students who might otherwise feel that a disaster occurring elsewhere has little connection to their own daily lives often report feeling genuinely involved once they are personally responsible for planning and running even a small stall.</u> Teachers who have run these fairs for years describe this sense of ownership as, in some respects, as valuable as the funds eventually raised.\n\n[I] Individual stalls rarely raise large sums on their own, a fact that occasionally discourages students before the fair begins. [II] Totals announced at the end of the day, however, frequently surprise students who had assumed their own modest contribution barely mattered. [III] This gap between individual expectation and combined outcome is, according to teachers, one of the fair's most memorable lessons. [IV] Several students have described this moment as changing how they think about collective action more generally.\n\nCritics occasionally argue that such fairs raise less money overall than a single well-organised donation campaign might. Organisers generally accept this trade-off, arguing that the broader educational value, specifically the lesson that modest individual contributions can combine into something <b>substantial</b>, justifies the comparatively smaller scale."
