        "answer": 0,
        "passage": "Transitioning from primary to secondary school remains, for many students, one of the more disorienting changes they experience during their education, involving unfamiliar routines, new academic expectations and an entirely new social environment all at once. Peer mentorship programmes, in which older students are paired with newcomers, are increasingly used by schools as a relatively <b>low-cost</b> way to ease this transition.\n\nWhat makes these programmes effective, according to coordinators who run them, often has little to do with academic tutoring itself. <u>A younger student struggling to adjust frequently benefits more from simply having an approachable older student to ask small, seemingly obvious questions than from any formal instruction a teacher might provide.</u> Mentors themselves often arrive expecting their main task to involve helping with homework, only to discover that most questions concern practical matters like finding classrooms or understanding school routines.\n\n[I] This mismatch between expectation and actual need initially surprises many new mentors. [II] Coordinators report that mentors typically adjust quickly once they realise how much simple reassurance matters to a nervous younger student. [III] Several mentors describe this realisation as unexpectedly changing how seriously they take the role. [IV] What began as a scheduling obligation often becomes, for many mentors, a source of genuine satisfaction by the end of the term.\n\nSchools considering whether to introduce such programmes often cite the lack of any need for specialised training as a significant advantage, since most of what mentors provide, patience, approachability and consistent availability, requires little more preparation than a short orientation and a genuine willingness to show up, however <b>ordinary</b> that commitment might initially seem."
