        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "Many people believe that Thomas Edison invented the light bulb, but the true story is more complicated. Several inventors had produced electric lights before him, yet their bulbs burned out quickly or were too <b>expensive</b> to use. Edison's achievement was to make a bulb that was practical, long-lasting and affordable for ordinary households.\n\nTo do this, Edison and his team tested many different materials for the thin wire, called the filament, inside the bulb. Bulbs made with certain materials lasted far longer than <b>those</b> made with others. <u>Rather than seeing each failure as a defeat, Edison treated it as useful information about what did not work.</u> In 1879, his team finally produced a bulb that glowed for many hours.\n\nThe success of the light bulb did not depend on a single idea. Edison also needed a reliable source of electricity, so his company built power stations and wires to deliver it to homes. In this way, he created not just a product but a whole system. Many historians consider this system to be as <b>significant</b> as the bulb itself.\n\nEdison's methods also remain influential. His laboratory employed engineers, craftsmen and mathematicians who worked together, an approach that resembles modern research teams. His belief that invention depends more on hard work than on sudden inspiration is often quoted today, but it should be remembered that success also required teamwork, money and a great deal of patience."
