        "passage": "Many people imagine that inventors work alone in a laboratory, planning every step of a project. In reality, a surprising number of useful products were <b>stumbled upon</b> by researchers who were looking for something entirely different. The microwave oven, the Post-it note and penicillin all began in this way.\n\nPercy Spencer, an American engineer, was testing a radar device in 1945 when he noticed that a chocolate bar in his pocket had melted. Curious, he placed some popcorn near the device, and it began to pop. Within a few years, the first microwave ovens had been built. Similarly, a scientist at the company 3M created a glue that was too weak to hold objects firmly together. <u>For many years nobody could find a purpose for the weak glue until a colleague used it to hold bookmarks in place.</u> This small idea eventually became the Post-it note, which is now sold in almost every country.\n\n[I] Of course, luck alone is not enough. [II] The people who made these discoveries were observant and willing to question what they saw. [III] Many other researchers might have ignored the melted chocolate or thrown away the useless glue. [IV] Alexander Fleming, for example, noticed that a mould had killed the bacteria around it in a dish he had forgotten to clean, and this observation led to the first antibiotic.\n\nToday, companies try to encourage this kind of discovery. Some allow employees to spend part of their working week on projects of their own choice, while others reward staff for ideas that did not succeed but taught the company something useful. They believe that a workplace which accepts mistakes is more likely to produce breakthroughs than <b>those</b> that punish them."
