        "passage": "School robotics competitions have grown rapidly in popularity over the past decade, with organisers reporting that the number of participating teams often doubles within just a few years of a competition's introduction at a new school. What draws students in, according to teachers who coach these teams, is rarely an existing interest in engineering alone, but rather the <b>tangible</b> nature of the challenge itself.\n\nUnlike many classroom assignments, a robotics project produces an outcome that either clearly works or clearly does not. <u>This immediate, visible feedback appears to motivate students in ways that a written assignment, graded weeks later, generally cannot replicate.</u> Coaches describe watching previously disengaged students suddenly invest considerable extra time refining a robot that stubbornly refuses to complete a simple task.\n\n[I] This hands-on frustration, rather than discouraging students, seems to deepen their investment in solving the problem. [II] Teams that fail early attempts often return the following day with renewed determination rather than reduced interest. [III] Coaches report that this persistence frequently surprises parents more accustomed to seeing their children give up quickly on difficult homework. [IV] Several coaches consider this shift in persistence more valuable long-term than any specific engineering skill the competition teaches.\n\nCritics occasionally argue that robotics competitions favour students who already have access to expensive kits or specialised equipment at home. Organisers generally respond by capping budgets and providing <b>standardised</b> parts to every team, arguing that creative problem-solving, not equipment cost, should determine which teams succeed."
