        "options": [
          "essential",
          "expensive",
          "common",
          "small"
        ],
        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "School clubs built around 3D printing have expanded rapidly as the equipment has become more affordable, yet teachers who run these clubs report a consistent pattern among new members that surprises almost nobody who has taught design before: a student's first printed object rarely resembles what they had pictured on screen. This gap between digital design and physical result, once considered a <b>minor</b> technical hiccup, is now treated by many instructors as an essential part of the learning process itself.\n\nWhat causes this gap, instructors explain, usually has less to do with a flawed original idea than with a lack of intuition for how digital measurements translate into a physical object. <u>A shape that looks perfectly proportioned on a flat screen can turn out, once printed, to be oddly thin, unexpectedly heavy, or simply the wrong size entirely.</u> Students typically need to print and adjust a design several times before developing a reliable sense of this translation.\n\n[I] New members often arrive expecting their first attempt to be close to final. [II] Discovering otherwise occasionally discourages a student before they have tried a second attempt. [III] Instructors who anticipate this reaction generally frame the first print as a deliberate test rather than a finished product. [IV] Reframed this way, most students approach their second attempt with noticeably less frustration than their first.\n\nSome parents question whether the cost of printing materials for repeated failed attempts is justified by the lessons learned. Instructors generally argue that developing this physical-digital intuition, rather than producing a single polished object, is the club's genuinely <b>valuable</b> outcome, one that transfers well beyond 3D printing itself."
