        "passage": "A student who joined her school's 3D printing club mainly out of curiosity designed a simple phone stand for her very first project, confident that such a basic shape would print without any complications. Her confidence proved <b>misplaced</b> when the finished print emerged warped and unable to support even the lightest phone without tipping over.\n\n<u>Rather than abandoning the project after this disappointing first attempt, she spent the following week researching why flat, thin shapes often warp during printing, eventually discovering that her design lacked sufficient thickness at its base to remain stable.</u> She redesigned the stand with a slightly thicker, wider base and printed it again.\n\nThe second attempt performed exactly as intended, holding a phone securely at a comfortable viewing angle. What the student found most <b>striking</b> about the experience, she later reflected, was not the eventual success itself but how much she had learned about structural stability simply by investigating one failed print closely.\n\nShe now encourages other new club members not to view a failed first print as evidence they lack any design talent, suggesting instead that investigating exactly why a design failed often teaches more than a successful first attempt ever could."
