        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "Competitions that restrict entrants to building something useful entirely from recycled materials have grown increasingly popular in schools, partly as a response to rising concern about waste, but also because teachers have noticed an unexpected side effect: entries built under strict material limits are often considered more creative, not less, than those built with unrestricted budgets. This pattern initially struck many teachers as <b>counterintuitive</b>, since fewer available options would seem to narrow rather than expand creative possibility.\n\nResearchers studying creativity under constraint offer one possible explanation. <u>When every possible material is available, students often default to familiar, conventional solutions, whereas a genuinely limited set of options forces them to consider combinations and uses they might never have attempted otherwise.</u> A broken umbrella frame, for instance, might never be considered as a structural support until a lack of other options makes it the only workable choice.\n\n[I] This forced reconsideration of ordinary objects appears to be where much of the creative value lies. [II] Students report noticing potential uses for discarded materials weeks after a challenge has ended, well beyond the competition itself. [III] Several teachers describe this shift in perception as more valuable than any single invention produced during the event. [IV] A student who once saw a broken umbrella frame as simply rubbish may now instinctively consider its structural possibilities.\n\nCritics occasionally argue that overly strict material limits unfairly disadvantage teams with less creative confidence from the outset. Organisers generally respond that the constraint itself, rather than disadvantaging anyone, tends to <b>level</b> the competition, since unlimited budgets would otherwise favour teams with greater access to expensive materials."
