        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Repair cafe events, where volunteers help members of the public fix broken household items rather than discard them, have spread from their origins in a handful of cities to schools and community centres in many countries. What draws volunteers to these events, according to organisers who coordinate them, is often less an interest in repair itself than a genuine curiosity about how ordinary devices work, a curiosity that formal education rarely has room to <b>satisfy</b> directly.\n\nThe appeal for item owners, meanwhile, tends to rest on a different kind of discovery. <u>Many owners arrive assuming their broken device is a lost cause, only to discover that the actual fault, once a volunteer opens the device and explains what has gone wrong, is often considerably simpler than they had imagined.</u> A worn switch or a loose wire frequently accounts for a failure the owner had assumed required an entirely new replacement.\n\n[I] This gap between assumed complexity and actual simplicity appears to be one of the event's most consistently reported surprises. [II] Owners who successfully participate in fixing their own item often describe feeling a disproportionate sense of accomplishment relative to the relatively minor fix involved. [III] Volunteers say this reaction rarely diminishes even among owners who have attended several previous repair events. [IV] Many owners report feeling noticeably more willing to attempt a simple repair themselves the next time something breaks.\n\nOrganisers emphasise that repair cafes exist primarily to reduce unnecessary waste, yet many consider this shift in owners' confidence, their growing <b>willingness</b> to open a broken device rather than immediately discard it, to be an equally significant outcome of the events."
