        "passage": "A student with a general interest in electronics volunteered at his school's first Repair Cafe Day, assigned to a table that quickly attracted a queue of visitors carrying broken items he had never personally attempted to fix before. His first repair attempt, an old electric fan that had stopped spinning, left him feeling genuinely <b>uncertain</b> about whether he could actually help.\n\n<u>Rather than guessing at a solution, he carefully disassembled the fan piece by piece in front of its owner, explaining aloud what each component appeared to do as he went, even when he was not yet certain what had actually failed.</u> This process eventually revealed a simple obstruction tangled around the motor shaft, one that took only moments to remove once identified.\n\nWord of this straightforward success spread quickly among the queue of waiting visitors, several of whom specifically requested his table afterward, assuming, somewhat to his own surprise, considerably more expertise than he actually possessed. He remained notably <b>modest</b> about the attention, insisting the fix had required patience rather than any specialised technical knowledge.\n\nHe now encourages other inexperienced volunteers not to assume a lack of formal expertise disqualifies them from helping, suggesting that methodically working through a problem aloud, rather than pretending to already know the answer, often matters more to a worried owner than any specific technical skill."
