        "passage": "School science fairs, long a familiar fixture of science education, continue to attract a wide variety of projects each year, ranging from elaborately equipped experiments to remarkably simple investigations conducted with ordinary household materials. Teachers who judge these events consistently report that the equipment involved correlates only <b>weakly</b> with how highly a project is ultimately scored, a finding that surprises many first-time presenters.\n\nWhat appears to matter considerably more, according to experienced judges, is the clarity with which a student can explain both their original question and what they ultimately learned from investigating it. <u>A student who clearly explains why a particular question interested them personally, and walks a judge logically through what they discovered, frequently outscores a technically elaborate project whose presenter struggles to articulate exactly what question the experiment was designed to answer.</u> This pattern holds remarkably consistently across different grade levels.\n\n[I] First-time presenters often assume that borrowing advanced laboratory equipment will automatically strengthen their project. [II] Experienced students, by contrast, frequently prioritise rehearsing a clear explanation over acquiring impressive equipment. [III] Judges report that this difference in preparation is usually immediately apparent during a presentation. [IV] A hesitant, unclear explanation can undermine an otherwise interesting experiment regardless of how sophisticated its equipment was.\n\nTeachers who coach first-time presenters therefore now emphasise rehearsing a project's explanation well before the showcase itself, arguing that this kind of communication practice, while seemingly unrelated to the science itself, is frequently what separates a <b>memorable</b> presentation from a forgettable one."
