        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "For many years, certain jobs were considered suitable only for men or only for women. Nursing and teaching were seen as women's work, while engineering and construction were thought to belong to men. These ideas, known as gender <b>stereotypes</b>, were rarely questioned, and they shaped what boys and girls hoped to become.\n\nToday, this picture is changing. More women are working as pilots, surgeons and company directors, and more men are choosing to become nurses, primary teachers and chefs. <u>Research shows that mixed teams, in which men and women work together, often make better decisions because they combine a wider range of experiences and viewpoints.</u> Companies that recognise this are making an effort to recruit and promote people on the basis of ability alone.\n\n[I] Progress, however, has been uneven. [II] In some workplaces, women still earn less than men for the same work, and they are less likely to be promoted to senior positions. [III] Others feel pressure to choose between their careers and their families. [IV] Men, too, may face criticism if they take time off to care for children.\n\nChanging attitudes begins at school. Teachers can avoid language that assumes certain subjects or careers are for one gender, and textbooks can show men and women in a variety of roles. Parents, too, can encourage children to follow their interests rather than expectations. When boys and girls grow up believing that they can do any job, <b>they</b> are more likely to choose careers that suit their talents."
