        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Sport has long been seen as a male activity. Boys are often given footballs and bicycles, while girls are given dolls, and the difference can shape how children think about what they are allowed to do. As a result, many girls lose interest in sport as they enter their teens, even though they enjoy it when they are younger.\n\nYet girls who take part in sport gain a great deal. They become fitter, learn to work in teams and develop confidence in their abilities. Girls who play sport are more likely to stay in education and to take on leadership roles than <b>those</b> who do not. <u>Success on the pitch or the track can change how girls see themselves and how others see them.</u>\n\nHowever, women's sport still receives far less attention than men's. Matches are shown less often on television, prize money is lower in many competitions, and talented girls may lack proper coaching or training facilities. Some sports organisations have begun to correct this <b>imbalance</b> by offering equal prize money and building more sports centres for girls. Such steps are <b>essential</b> if girls are to have the same chances as boys.\n\nFamilies and schools can help too. Parents can encourage daughters to join teams and attend their matches, and schools can offer a wider choice of sports and make sure that girls' teams receive the same support as boys' teams. When young people see women athletes treated with respect, they learn that sport belongs to everyone."
