        "passage": "For centuries, science was considered a <b>male-dominated</b> field. Women who wanted to study physics, chemistry or medicine often had to overcome legal barriers as well as social prejudice, and many were refused places at universities simply because of their gender. Those who did succeed were frequently denied credit for their work.\n\nMarie Curie is perhaps the best-known example of a woman who triumphed over these obstacles. Born in Poland in 1867, she moved to Paris to continue her studies because women were not allowed to attend university in her own country. She went on to win two Nobel Prizes, in physics and in chemistry, becoming the first person to receive the award in two different sciences. <u>Her achievements did not only advance scientific knowledge but also encouraged other women to believe that they, too, could succeed in the laboratory.</u> Even so, she was never elected to the French Academy of Sciences, a fact that shows how slowly attitudes changed.\n\n[I] Today, the situation has improved, although it is far from perfect. [II] More girls than ever study science at school, and in several countries women now make up the majority of medical students. [III] However, they are still under-represented in fields such as engineering and computer science. [IV] Researchers suggest that this gap is partly the result of stereotypes that persuade girls early in life that these subjects are not meant for them.\n\nMany organisations now run programmes that give young women the chance to meet <b>role models</b> working in science and technology. Schools are also revising textbooks that once showed only men as scientists. Such measures cannot change attitudes overnight, but <b>they</b> send a clear message: talent has no gender, and societies lose out when they ignore half of their population."
