        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "Workshops designed to introduce young women to careers in science, technology, engineering and mathematics have become increasingly common in schools, driven partly by persistent statistics showing that women remain significantly underrepresented in many of these fields despite comparable academic performance. Organisers of such workshops argue that this gap has less to do with ability than with a lack of <b>visible</b> role models during the years when students begin forming assumptions about which careers suit them.\n\nResearch on career aspiration appears to support this reasoning. <u>Students who have personally met a professional working in a field they had never previously considered are measurably more likely to report that career as a realistic future option for themselves, compared with students who have only read about such professionals in textbooks.</u> This effect appears particularly pronounced when the professional shares an identity, such as gender, with the student.\n\n[I] Many participants in these workshops report having never personally known a woman working in engineering or computer science before attending. [II] Meeting someone in that role, rather than simply hearing statistics about underrepresentation, seems to shift how participants imagine their own future options. [III] Several participants describe this shift as considerably more persuasive than any career guidance pamphlet they had previously encountered. [IV] Workshop organisers consider this personal connection, rather than information alone, to be the programme's central mechanism.\n\nCritics occasionally question whether a single workshop can meaningfully influence a major career decision years later. Organisers generally acknowledge this limitation, arguing instead that the workshop's primary aim is simply to introduce an option that <b>otherwise</b> might never have occurred to a student at all."
