        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "Career days that deliberately feature speakers working outside the job roles traditionally associated with their gender have become a recognisable, if still relatively uncommon, feature of secondary school programming. Organisers of such events often cite a specific motivation: statistics showing persistent gender imbalance in certain professions tend to produce far less impact on student attitudes than a single compelling personal account from someone who has actually lived the experience.\n\nWhat distinguishes an effective speaker at these events, according to teachers who organise them repeatedly, is less professional accomplishment than <b>candour</b> about the specific moments their career choice was questioned by others. <u>A male nurse who openly describes a relative's initial disapproval, or a female electrician who recalls being underestimated by early clients, appears to resonate with students far more than a speaker who focuses solely on technical job responsibilities.</u> This personal vulnerability, rather than professional polish, seems to be what students remember afterward.\n\n[I] Students frequently arrive at these events expecting a fairly standard career presentation. [II] The unexpectedly personal nature of these talks often catches them somewhat off guard. [III] Several students report feeling more comfortable afterward discussing their own unconventional career interests with friends or family. [IV] Teachers consider this increased openness, rather than any specific career statistic learned, to be the event's most valuable outcome.\n\nSome parents have questioned whether schools should address gender stereotypes directly within career guidance programming at all. Organisers generally respond that career guidance, by its nature, already involves discussing which paths feel realistically <b>available</b> to a given student, making an honest conversation about gender-related assumptions a natural and necessary part of that same discussion."
