        "passage": "School home economics programmes, once routinely assumed to attract mainly female students, have in recent years been deliberately redesigned by some schools to actively recruit students of every gender, often through competitive events framed as a genuinely shared challenge rather than an optional elective. Teachers who organise these events report a pattern that initially surprised them: once male students are specifically and visibly recruited rather than merely permitted to participate, enrolment shifts considerably more than a passive, gender-neutral announcement alone tends to achieve.\n\nThis pattern reflects something researchers studying gendered assumptions in education have long observed elsewhere. <u>A subject technically open to everyone can still feel, through subtle cues in how it is promoted or who typically attends, implicitly reserved for one gender, discouraging interested students from the other gender from ever seriously considering it.</u> Deliberately recruiting male students specifically, rather than passively allowing them to attend, appears to disrupt this implicit assumption more effectively than neutral framing alone.\n\n[I] Male students who do attend such challenges often report feeling initially self-conscious about an activity they associate with one gender. [II] This self-consciousness, teachers note, tends to fade considerably once the activity begins and attention shifts to the actual cooking task. [III] Several male participants describe feeling surprised at how quickly the activity itself overrides any gendered expectation they had originally carried into the room. [IV] Teachers consider this shift in attention, from identity to task, central to why these challenges succeed where passive invitations typically fail.\n\nSkeptics occasionally question whether a single competitive event can meaningfully shift deeply held assumptions about gendered chores at home. Teachers generally acknowledge this limitation, while maintaining that even a modest shift in <b>comfort</b> with an activity may matter considerably over a student's lifetime."
