        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Events celebrating women's achievements in competitive sport have become a familiar feature of school programming in many countries, yet organisers who run these events increasingly emphasise a specific detail that surprises most student audiences: several restrictions on women's participation in major competitive sports were removed far more recently than most students initially assume. This historical <b>proximity</b>, organisers argue, matters considerably more to how students perceive current achievements than a vague, general celebration alone would.\n\nWhat tends to produce this surprise, teachers who organise these events explain, is a basic misunderstanding of timeline rather than any lack of awareness that barriers once existed. <u>Students generally know, in broad terms, that women faced historical exclusion from certain competitive sports, but frequently assume this exclusion ended decades earlier than the specific dates actually show.</u> Presenting these dates explicitly, rather than referring to the history only in general terms, appears to shift how students evaluate the achievements of current athletes.\n\n[I] Learning exactly how recent certain restrictions were tends to recontextualise a student's view of current athletic records. [II] An achievement that seemed simply impressive before can appear considerably more remarkable once students understand how recently the opportunity to compete was even available. [III] Several students report feeling genuinely surprised that relatives still living today witnessed some of these changes directly. [IV] This generational proximity, rather than abstract historical fact, appears to be what makes the information memorable.\n\nSome critics argue that focusing heavily on historical restriction risks overshadowing a celebration that should instead focus entirely on present-day achievement. Organisers generally respond that understanding this recent history, far from overshadowing current achievement, actually deepens appreciation for it, since context rather than celebration alone is what makes an achievement feel genuinely <b>significant</b>."
