        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "Household surveys asking students to record exactly who performs specific domestic tasks consistently produce a finding that researchers describe as remarkably stable across different communities: most respondents initially estimate their household's division of chores as considerably more even than a detailed, task-by-task accounting ultimately reveals. This gap between perception and documented reality, researchers argue, reflects something more interesting than simple dishonesty on the part of respondents.\n\nWhat appears to drive this gap, according to sociologists who study household labour, is less a conscious misrepresentation than a genuine difficulty noticing unevenly distributed tasks without deliberately counting them. <u>Tasks performed frequently but briefly, such as reminding a sibling to do homework or checking whether school uniforms are clean, tend to go unnoticed as actual labour precisely because they rarely take the dramatic, visible form associated with chores like cooking a full meal.</u> A detailed survey, by forcing respondents to list every task individually, makes this previously invisible labour considerably harder to overlook.\n\n[I] Students completing such surveys often report a genuine moment of surprise partway through the list. [II] Tasks they had never previously considered worth mentioning suddenly appear, once written down, to add up to a significant portion of household labour. [III] This written exercise, rather than any outside argument, frequently proves more persuasive to sceptical respondents than a verbal explanation of the same pattern. [IV] Researchers consider this self-discovered realisation considerably more durable than any conclusion simply told to a respondent directly.\n\nCritics occasionally argue that survey-based self-reporting remains an unreliable method for measuring actual household labour. Researchers generally acknowledge this limitation while maintaining that the exercise's educational value, prompting respondents to notice a pattern they had never consciously examined, matters regardless of the data's perfect <b>precision</b>."
