        "passage": "Awareness campaigns addressing why some rural families continue to prioritise a son's education over a daughter's face a particular challenge that organisers say requires careful handling: presenting the issue accurately risks sounding like a direct criticism of individual families, when the pattern researchers actually document is driven considerably more by economic pressure than by a simple preference for one gender over another. Volunteers researching the topic are specifically trained to make this distinction clear in every presentation.\n\nWhat research on the subject generally shows, according to education researchers who study rural school enrolment, is that limited household income, rather than straightforward favouritism, frequently forces families into difficult choices between children's schooling. <u>When a family can only afford to keep one child in secondary school, research suggests this choice is often shaped by which child is expected to eventually earn more outside the home, a calculation shaped by broader economic patterns rather than a family's personal affection for either child.</u> Scholarship programmes targeting girls specifically aim to remove this forced calculation entirely.\n\n[I] Volunteers researching this topic for the first time often initially assume the pattern reflects simple personal preference within individual families. [II] Reading detailed research accounts typically shifts this assumption considerably. [III] Many volunteers report feeling noticeably more careful about how they phrase their presentations once they understand the economic pressures involved. [IV] This shift in framing, organisers argue, makes audiences considerably more receptive than a more judgemental presentation would achieve.\n\nSome critics argue that framing the issue primarily around economics risks downplaying genuine gender bias that exists in some households regardless of income. Organisers generally acknowledge that both factors can coexist, while maintaining that leading with economic context makes the issue's genuinely <b>structural</b> nature clearer to an audience than a framing centred solely on individual attitudes would."
