        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "The International Red Cross and Red Crescent Movement is one of the largest humanitarian networks in the world. It began in 1863, when a group of citizens in Switzerland decided that wounded soldiers should be cared for, whichever side they fought on. Today, the movement helps victims of wars, floods, earthquakes and epidemics in almost every country.\n\nIn Viet Nam, the Red Cross Society, founded in 1946, runs programmes in disaster relief, blood donation and care for disadvantaged people. During floods and storms, its volunteers deliver food, clean water and medicine to families in affected areas. Villages where volunteers have been trained usually recover faster than <b>those</b> that have received no preparation. <u>Teaching people how to respond before a disaster happens can save as many lives as sending help afterwards.</u>\n\nThe movement is guided by seven principles, including humanity, neutrality and independence. Neutrality means that it does not take sides in conflicts, so that its workers can reach people in need on every side. This approach is not always easy to maintain, and workers sometimes face danger. Yet it explains why the Red Cross is widely <b>trusted</b> by governments and ordinary people alike.\n\nYoung people can join the movement through youth branches in schools and universities. They learn first aid, take part in blood drives and help to organise community events. Such activities teach teenagers that humanitarian work does not require special qualifications, only willingness and <b>commitment</b>."
