        "passage": "Every year, thousands of students take part in exchange programmes that send them abroad for several months. Although living in a foreign country can be exciting, it is rarely easy at first. Students must adapt to a new language, new food and unfamiliar customs, and many feel lonely during the first weeks.\n\nPsychologists describe this experience as culture shock, a feeling of confusion that appears when people are removed from familiar surroundings. It usually passes in stages. At first, everything seems fascinating; later, small differences become irritating; finally, the students learn to accept the new culture and feel at home. <u>Students who keep an open mind and are willing to laugh at their own mistakes generally overcome these difficulties more easily.</u> Those who prepare in advance by studying the language and customs of the host country usually recover faster than <b>those</b> who do not.\n\nThe benefits of the experience are <b>considerable</b>. Exchange students often return with better language skills, but the most important changes are personal. They become more independent, learn to solve problems on their own and develop a deeper understanding of people from other backgrounds. Employers value these qualities, which is why a year abroad can improve a young person's <b>prospects</b> of finding a good job.\n\nOf course, an exchange programme is not suitable for everyone. It can be expensive, and some families cannot afford the cost. For this reason, many schools now offer scholarships, and some organise online exchanges in which students from different countries work together on projects without leaving their classrooms. Although these virtual programmes cannot fully replace living abroad, they allow more young people to experience other cultures at a lower cost."
