        "passage": "A group of students assigned to build a model of a Vietnamese UNESCO heritage site initially planned to focus entirely on the site's most photographed, visually impressive features, assuming these would make for the most immediately impressive final model. Their teacher's feedback on an early sketch left them feeling somewhat <b>deflated</b>, as she pointed out that their design said nothing about why the site had actually earned its recognition in the first place.\n\n<u>Rather than simply adding a few explanatory labels to their original visual design, the group decided to research the specific conservation criteria the site had satisfied and rebuild significant parts of their model around demonstrating these criteria directly, even where doing so made the model noticeably less visually striking than their original plan.</u> This decision required discarding several hours of earlier work.\n\nThe group's eventual presentation, while admittedly less immediately eye-catching than some competing models, received markedly more detailed and <b>favourable</b> feedback from judges than their original approach likely would have earned, with judges specifically praising how clearly the model demonstrated the site's actual conservation significance rather than merely its visual appeal.\n\nThe group now advises other students working on similar projects not to assume visual impressiveness alone will satisfy judges, suggesting that clearly demonstrating why a site matters, even at some cost to visual polish, often earns more genuine credit than an impressive but substantively empty presentation."
