        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Workshops introducing students to international agreements on children's rights often face a specific communication challenge that facilitators say has little to do with the topic's importance: the formal legal language in which such agreements are typically written tends to feel genuinely distant from a student's own daily experience, even when the rights themselves apply directly to that experience. Bridging this gap, facilitators argue, matters considerably more to a workshop's effectiveness than simply covering the agreement's content <b>comprehensively</b>.\n\nWhat appears to close this gap most effectively, according to facilitators who run these workshops repeatedly, is replacing abstract legal phrasing with specific, immediately recognisable daily scenarios. <u>Rather than explaining a right to rest and leisure in formal terms, an effective facilitator might instead ask students to consider whether they ever feel they have too little free time, connecting an abstract legal provision directly to a feeling most students have personally experienced.</u> This technique appears to measurably increase how much students report remembering from a session afterward.\n\n[I] Students initially exposed only to the agreement's formal text often describe the material as relevant mainly to children in clearly difficult circumstances elsewhere. [II] Once concrete, locally relevant examples are introduced, many students begin recognising rights they had never previously thought to frame in that way. [III] This shift in recognition, facilitators report, often happens within a single session rather than requiring extended exposure. [IV] Several facilitators consider this immediate shift in perspective the workshop's single most valuable outcome.\n\nSome educators question whether simplifying formal legal language for a school audience risks losing important nuance contained in the original agreement. Facilitators generally argue that a <b>simplified</b> but genuinely understood version of a right serves students considerably better than a precisely worded but largely unremembered one."
