        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Students preparing displays about global sustainable development goals often assume, based on how these goals are typically summarised in international reports, that the targets mainly describe conditions in distant countries facing severe poverty, with little direct relevance to their own community. Educators who run these fairs consistently note that this assumption significantly <b>understates</b> how directly several of the goals connect to ordinary local conditions, including water quality, waste management and access to green space.\n\nWhat makes this local connection genuinely difficult for students to see at first, according to educators, is less a lack of relevant local examples than the formal, global-scale language in which the goals are typically written. <u>A goal framed in terms of national or international statistics can feel considerably more abstract than the same underlying issue described through a specific local example, such as a nearby river's water quality or a local market's waste disposal practices.</u> Students who research a concrete local example, educators report, tend to produce noticeably stronger displays.\n\n[I] Students initially exposed only to the goals' formal global statistics often describe the material as relevant mainly to distant, severely affected communities. [II] Once a specific local example is researched and incorporated, many students begin recognising the goal's direct relevance to their own daily surroundings. [III] This shift in recognition, educators report, often happens within a single research session rather than requiring extended exposure. [IV] Several educators consider this immediate shift in perspective the fair's single most valuable outcome.\n\nSome critics argue that connecting global goals to small local examples risks trivialising issues that are, at a global scale, extremely serious. Educators generally respond that a <b>locally grounded</b> but genuinely understood version of a goal serves students considerably better than an abstractly worded but largely unremembered one."
