        "passage": "A group of students assigned to build a display about reducing poverty initially planned to focus entirely on statistics describing poverty in distant countries, assuming this global framing would best demonstrate the seriousness of the issue. Their teacher's feedback on an early draft left them feeling somewhat <b>deflated</b>, as she pointed out that their display said nothing about how the goal connected to conditions in their own town.\n\n<u>Rather than simply adding a line mentioning their town, the group decided to research local poverty-related data, such as food bank usage and affordable housing availability, and rebuilt significant parts of their display around these specific local findings.</u> This decision required discarding several hours of earlier research.\n\nThe group's eventual presentation, while admittedly less dramatic than their original global statistics, received markedly more detailed and <b>favourable</b> feedback from judges than their original approach likely would have earned, with judges specifically praising how clearly the display connected a global goal to conditions students could recognise directly.\n\nThe group now advises other students working on similar displays not to assume global statistics alone will fully engage an audience, suggesting that clearly connecting a goal to local, recognisable conditions, even at some cost to dramatic scale, often earns more genuine credit than an impressive but distant-feeling presentation."
