        "passage": "In recent years, online learning has grown from a minor option into a mainstream way of studying. Students can join live video lessons, watch recorded lectures and complete exercises on platforms that give instant feedback. This <b>flexibility</b> is especially valuable for students who live far from good schools or who need to combine study with other responsibilities.\n\nOnline learning also allows each student to work at an individual pace. A learner who finds a topic difficult can replay a video as many times as necessary, while another who understands quickly can move on at once. <u>This control over time and speed can make learning more efficient, particularly in subjects that need repeated practice.</u> Many platforms also keep a record of progress, so that students can see how much they have achieved.\n\n[I] Nevertheless, online learning has its drawbacks. [II] Without a teacher present, some students find it hard to stay focused, especially when notifications and games are only a click away. [III] Others miss the friendships and spontaneous discussions of a real classroom. [IV] Poor internet connections can also interrupt lessons, putting students in remote areas at a disadvantage.\n\nEducators therefore recommend a blended approach, in which online activities are combined with face-to-face teaching. Students who follow such programmes can enjoy the convenience of digital tools while still receiving guidance from teachers. Schools that plan carefully and train their staff well tend to achieve better results than <b>those</b> that simply replace classes with videos."
