        "passage": "Imagine a class that spends several weeks designing a plan to reduce waste in its school. Instead of memorising facts from a textbook, students research the problem, interview people and present their solutions. This approach, known as project-based learning, is becoming popular in schools around the world.\n\nTeachers who use projects say that students remember lessons longer than <b>those</b> who study only from textbooks. <u>Because the work involves real problems, students see the purpose of what they learn and take more responsibility for it.</u> Projects also develop skills such as teamwork, research and public speaking, which employers value highly.\n\nYet project-based learning is not easy to organise. It requires time, careful planning and flexible timetables, and some students find open tasks <b>daunting</b> because there is no single correct answer. Marking can also be difficult, since teachers must judge both the final product and the effort that went into it.\n\nSuccessful projects usually share several features. They begin with a clear question, give students some choice and include regular feedback from teachers and classmates. At the end, students present their work to an audience, which gives them a sense of <b>achievement</b>."
