        "passage": "A few decades ago, studying at a university meant moving to a campus, attending lectures in person and paying high fees. Today, millions of people can follow courses taught by famous professors without leaving their homes. These courses, known as MOOCs (massive open online courses), have <b>transformed</b> the way knowledge is shared around the world.\n\nThe first MOOCs appeared in the early 2010s, and their numbers have grown steadily since then. Most of them are free or cheap, and anyone with an internet connection can enrol. Learners watch short video lectures, take part in online discussions and complete quizzes that are marked automatically. <u>Because the courses are open to everyone, they offer valuable opportunities to people who could not otherwise afford higher education.</u> Some universities and employers now recognise certificates from these courses.\n\n[I] However, MOOCs are not without problems. [II] Studies show that only a small percentage of learners complete the courses they begin. [III] Without a teacher to remind them of deadlines, many people lose motivation or find that they cannot manage their time. [IV] Others struggle because they have no one to ask when they do not understand something.\n\nTo address these problems, course designers are introducing study groups, mentors and personalised feedback. Some platforms use artificial intelligence to recommend lessons that suit each learner's level. Experts believe that, in the future, online and classroom learning will be combined, and that the most successful students will be <b>those</b> who can take responsibility for their own progress."
