        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Almost every student has experienced the temptation to put off an important task until the last minute. Psychologists call this habit procrastination, and they stress that it has little to do with laziness. In most cases, students delay work because it makes them feel anxious or bored, and avoiding it gives them a short-term feeling of relief.\n\nThe problem is that this relief rarely lasts. As the deadline approaches, stress increases, and the quality of the work usually suffers. <u>Students who regularly postpone their assignments tend to sleep less and feel more worried than <b>those</b> who start early.</u> Over time, the habit can damage their results and their confidence.\n\nFortunately, there are practical ways to break the cycle. Experts advise dividing large tasks into small, <b>manageable</b> steps so that the work no longer seems overwhelming. Setting a specific time and place for study also helps, since the brain begins to link that environment with concentration. Switching off a phone during study sessions removes one of the most common distractions.\n\nEqually important is the way students treat themselves. Researchers have found that those who forgive themselves for past delays are less likely to <b>repeat</b> the behaviour than those who criticise themselves harshly. A positive attitude gives students the energy to start again, whereas guilt tends to make the task seem even more unpleasant."
