        "passage": "Students encountering a flipped classroom for the first time often assume, based on how the approach is typically described in brief announcements, that its main feature is simply relocating lecture content from the classroom to a video watched at home, with the classroom experience otherwise remaining largely unchanged. Teachers who have implemented flipped classrooms consistently note that this assumption significantly <b>understates</b> how much the actual classroom experience changes once lecture content moves elsewhere.\n\nWhat changes most substantially, according to teachers who have made this shift, is not where information is first encountered but what students actually do with class time once freed from passive listening. <u>Class time that would otherwise be spent delivering a lecture can instead be devoted to discussion, collaborative problem-solving and individualised feedback, activities that a traditional lecture format leaves comparatively little room for.</u> This reallocation, teachers argue, is the approach's genuine core, not merely a side effect of moving videos home.\n\n[I] Students who skip the home video sometimes assume they can simply absorb the material passively during the following class discussion. [II] Teachers have increasingly found that this assumption leaves such students considerably less able to participate meaningfully. [III] Several teachers have responded by adding brief comprehension checks at the start of class to confirm preparation. [IV] This adjustment appears to have measurably improved how prepared students are for in-class discussion.\n\nCritics occasionally argue that flipped classrooms simply shift the burden of learning onto students' own time without proper support. Defenders generally respond that this view underestimates how much more individualised support students actually receive during class once the teacher is <b>freed</b> from lecturing."
