        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "Students encountering a peer tutoring app for the first time often assume, based on how such programmes are typically introduced in school announcements, that the service exists mainly for students who are significantly behind in a subject and need substantial remedial help. Teachers who have observed these apps in actual use consistently note that this assumption significantly <b>understates</b> how broadly students of all ability levels end up using the service.\n\nWhat actual usage data from similar programmes tends to show, according to teachers who track it, is that a large proportion of sessions involve students seeking quick clarification on a single confusing point rather than comprehensive remedial support. <u>A student who generally performs well in a subject may still book a short session simply to clarify one specific homework question, a use case quite different from the sustained remedial support the app's name might initially suggest.</u> This pattern, teachers argue, helps explain why usage often extends well beyond struggling students alone.\n\n[I] Some students initially hesitate to use the app, worried that booking a session might be seen by classmates as a sign of struggling academically. [II] Teachers have increasingly emphasised in school announcements that confident students use the service just as often as anyone else. [III] This reframing appears to have measurably increased how willing students are to book a session. [IV] Teachers consider this increased willingness one of the more significant outcomes of correcting the initial assumption.\n\nCritics occasionally argue that peer tutoring apps cannot replace the expertise of a trained adult teacher. Defenders generally acknowledge this limitation, while pointing out that the app's real value lies in its <b>immediate</b> availability for small, specific questions that would otherwise go unresolved for hours or days."
