        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "Students encountering a virtual reality science lab for the first time often assume, based on how the technology is typically presented in promotional videos, that its main appeal lies in making experiments feel more visually exciting than their real-world equivalents. Science teachers who have used these simulations in actual classrooms consistently note that this assumption significantly <b>understates</b> the technology's genuine educational value, which lies elsewhere entirely.\n\nWhat gives these simulations real educational value, according to teachers who use them regularly, is the ability to let students repeat a procedure as many times as needed to fully understand it, something a real laboratory setting, constrained by cost, time and safety concerns, simply cannot offer for genuinely dangerous experiments. <u>A student who misunderstands a reaction's mechanism after watching it once can simply repeat the simulation immediately, an option a real explosive or toxic reaction would never safely allow.</u> This repeatability, teachers argue, often matters considerably more to actual learning than any visual enhancement.\n\n[I] Some teachers initially worried that students might treat a risk-free simulation too casually, failing to take the underlying chemistry seriously. [II] In practice, many teachers have found that repeated simulation attempts actually deepen a student's engagement with the underlying mechanism rather than trivialising it. [III] Several teachers now report noticeably improved conceptual understanding among students who use the repeatable simulation compared with those who only watched a single demonstration. [IV] This improved understanding is considered one of the simulation's most significant, if initially unexpected, benefits.\n\nCritics occasionally argue that virtual simulations cannot fully replace the tactile, sensory experience of real laboratory work. Defenders generally acknowledge this limitation, while pointing out that for experiments too dangerous to attempt safely at all, a <b>repeatable</b> simulation is considerably better than no hands-on experience whatsoever."
