        "passage": "Students encountering a gamified vocabulary app for the first time often assume, based on how such apps are typically advertised, that points, badges and certificates represent the primary mechanism driving regular practice. Researchers who study gamified learning tools consistently note that this assumption significantly <b>understates</b> how much the social, team-based elements of these apps actually contribute to sustained engagement.\n\nWhat seems to matter most for keeping students practising consistently, according to researchers who study usage patterns, is not the individual reward itself but the visible, shared stake in a team or class outcome. <u>A student who might easily skip an individual practice session if only their own score were at stake often continues practising specifically because a class's shared leaderboard position depends partly on their continued participation.</u> This social accountability, researchers argue, often outweighs any individual reward in sustaining long-term engagement.\n\n[I] App designers initially expected individual badges and certificates to be the main driver of continued practice. [II] Usage data from several schools instead showed noticeably higher engagement on apps featuring team or class-based competition elements. [III] This finding led several designers to redesign their apps around shared, visible team outcomes rather than purely individual achievement. [IV] Researchers consider this redesign one of the more significant shifts in how gamified learning tools are now built.\n\nCritics occasionally argue that team-based competition in educational apps can create unhealthy pressure on struggling students who feel responsible for their team's performance. Designers generally acknowledge this risk, while arguing that a well-designed app can make the <b>shared</b> element feel supportive rather than purely competitive, by rewarding team participation broadly rather than individual scores narrowly."
