        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "Students assigned a podcast project for the first time often assume, based on how casual and conversational finished podcasts typically sound, that recording a spoken explanation is inherently easier than producing a carefully written one. Teachers who have supervised these projects consistently note that this assumption significantly <b>understates</b> how much genuine understanding a clear spoken explanation actually requires.\n\nWhat makes spoken explanation genuinely demanding, according to teachers who have observed many student recordings, is that a listener cannot pause to reread a confusing sentence the way a reader can. <u>A written explanation that loses clarity partway through can still be understood on a second reading, but a listener who loses the thread of a spoken explanation has, in practice, already lost the explanation entirely.</u> This difference, teachers argue, makes spoken clarity a considerably higher bar than written clarity in some respects.\n\n[I] Groups recording their first episode often attempt to speak entirely from memory without any prior script or outline. [II] Many groups discover during this first attempt that speaking from memory alone produces noticeably less organised explanations than they expected. [III] Teachers have increasingly encouraged groups to prepare a rough outline, though not a full script, before recording. [IV] This preparation step appears to measurably improve how clearly a group's recorded explanation comes across.\n\nCritics occasionally argue that scripting a podcast episode in advance makes it sound less natural and conversational than an unscripted recording. Defenders generally acknowledge this trade-off, while arguing that a loosely structured outline achieves <b>clarity</b> without sacrificing the natural, conversational tone that makes a podcast engaging to listen to."
