        "passage": "Students encountering an AI writing feedback tool for the first time often assume, based on how instantly and confidently the tool delivers its comments, that it can substitute fully for a teacher's feedback on an essay draft. Teachers who have used these tools alongside their own feedback consistently note that this assumption significantly <b>overestimates</b> what the tool is actually capable of identifying.\n\nWhat these tools reliably catch, according to teachers who have compared both kinds of feedback closely, tends to be grammar errors, awkward phrasing and structural inconsistencies, issues with a relatively clear, rule-based correct answer. <u>A tool can confidently flag a subject-verb agreement error or a missing comma, but it struggles considerably more to judge whether an essay's central argument is actually convincing or whether evidence has been used persuasively.</u> This gap, teachers argue, is precisely where a teacher's feedback remains indispensable.\n\n[I] Some students initially treat the tool's feedback as a complete substitute, submitting a draft once the tool stops flagging errors. [II] Teachers have increasingly found that such drafts often still contain significant weaknesses in argument or evidence that the tool simply did not detect. [III] This finding has led several teachers to require a second, teacher-reviewed draft even after the tool's feedback has been addressed. [IV] This additional step appears to measurably improve the quality of arguments in final submissions.\n\nCritics occasionally argue that relying on an automated tool at all risks making students less attentive to their own writing. Defenders generally respond that using the tool to handle <b>mechanical</b> errors efficiently actually frees up class time for teachers to focus feedback on the deeper argument and evidence issues that matter more."
