        "passage": "Students planning revision schedules for the first time often assume, based on a simple intuition that more time spent studying should produce more learning, that a single long, uninterrupted study session is more efficient than splitting the same total time across several shorter ones. Researchers who study attention and memory consistently note that this assumption significantly <b>overestimates</b> how effectively a long, unbroken session actually converts time into lasting learning.\n\nWhat research on attention spans actually shows, according to researchers who study this area, is that sustained focus tends to decline measurably after a certain period, meaning the final portion of a long session often produces considerably less learning per minute than the earlier portion did. <u>A student who studies continuously for two hours may absorb most of what they will retain within roughly the first half of that time, with much of the remaining time contributing comparatively little additional learning.</u> This diminishing return, researchers argue, is precisely what splitting study time into shorter sessions is designed to avoid.\n\n[I] Students trying microlearning for the first time sometimes worry that short sessions feel too brief to cover meaningful material. [II] Researchers have found that the key advantage lies not in each session's length but in returning to the material repeatedly over time, which strengthens memory more than one extended exposure. [III] This repeated-exposure effect appears to measurably improve long-term retention compared with a single long session covering the same material once. [IV] Researchers consider this retention advantage one of microlearning's most well-supported benefits.\n\nCritics occasionally argue that short sessions make it harder to engage deeply with complex material requiring sustained concentration. Defenders generally acknowledge this limitation for certain tasks, while arguing that for most routine revision, the <b>repeated</b> exposure microlearning provides outweighs the benefit of sustained concentration in a single long session."
