        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "Parents encountering the term blended learning for the first time often assume, based on how heavily screens and devices feature in general discussions of modern education, that the approach mainly involves replacing a significant portion of traditional classroom teaching with online video lessons. Teachers who implement blended learning in their own classrooms consistently note that this assumption significantly <b>overstates</b> how much actual teaching time moves away from the classroom itself.\n\nWhat blended learning typically changes, according to teachers who use it, is less the balance between classroom and online time than the specific role each plays in a student's overall learning. <u>The classroom typically remains the primary setting for introducing new concepts through discussion and guided practice, while the online platform mainly supports independent homework practice, quick comprehension checks and additional resources for students who want extra help.</u> This division of roles, teachers argue, is considerably more balanced than the term blended learning might initially suggest to an unfamiliar parent.\n\n[I] Some parents initially worry that blended learning will significantly reduce how much direct, in-person teaching their child receives. [II] Teachers have increasingly used open house events specifically to demonstrate how classroom discussion remains central to the approach. [III] This demonstration appears to measurably reduce parental concern about excessive screen time once parents see the platform's actual role. [IV] Teachers consider this reduced concern one of the open house's most valuable outcomes.\n\nCritics occasionally argue that even a supporting role for online platforms risks increasing a student's overall screen time beyond what is healthy. Teachers generally acknowledge this concern is reasonable, while pointing out that the online platform's <b>supporting</b>, rather than central, role is specifically designed to keep additional screen time modest and purposeful."
