        "passage": "Air pollution is one of the most serious environmental problems in big cities. Vehicles, factories and the burning of rubbish release smoke, dust and <b>harmful</b> gases into the air. On some days, the sky looks grey and people can hardly see tall buildings in the distance.\n\nBreathing polluted air affects health in many ways. Fine dust can enter the lungs and cause coughing, asthma and other breathing problems. <u>Children and elderly people are especially at risk because their bodies are less able to cope with polluted air.</u> Doctors also report that long-term exposure may increase the risk of heart disease.\n\n[I] Governments can reduce air pollution by setting limits on emissions from factories and vehicles. [II] Many cities have introduced stricter rules for older motorbikes and cars. [III] Others have created low-emission zones, where the most polluting vehicles are not allowed to enter. [IV] Public transport, cycling lanes and electric buses are also being expanded to offer cleaner alternatives.\n\nIndividuals can also help. Walking, cycling or taking the bus for short trips reduces the number of vehicles on the road, and avoiding the burning of rubbish keeps smoke out of the air. Wearing a mask on days when pollution is high can offer some protection, but experts stress that <b>it</b> is only a temporary measure and cannot replace cleaner air."
