        "passage": "Coral reefs cover less than one per cent of the ocean floor, yet they support about a quarter of all marine species. For this reason, they are often described as the 'rainforests of the sea'. Millions of people also <b>depend on</b> reefs for food, income from tourism and protection against storms.\n\nUnfortunately, reefs are under serious threat. Rising sea temperatures cause corals to expel the tiny algae that give them colour and energy, a process known as bleaching. Pollution from farms and cities, destructive fishing methods and careless tourism add to the damage. <u>Scientists estimate that if present trends continue, most of the world's reefs could be lost within the next fifty years.</u> Once a reef has died, it may take decades or even centuries to recover.\n\n[I] Fortunately, there are signs of hope. [II] Some countries have created marine protected areas where fishing and building are strictly limited. [III] Volunteers also grow young corals in nurseries and then attach them to damaged reefs. [IV] These efforts have already helped several reefs to begin recovering.\n\nIndividuals can help, too. Tourists can choose operators that follow environmental rules, use sunscreen that does not harm corals, and avoid touching living reef organisms. Reducing the use of plastics and saving energy at home also helps. Such changes may seem small, but when millions of people make them, <b>they</b> can add up to a real difference."
