        "passage": "Students encountering a school composting programme for the first time often assume, based on how little attention is typically paid to what happens after a bin is emptied, that food waste simply disappears harmlessly once it leaves the canteen. Environmental educators who run these programmes consistently note that this assumption significantly <b>understates</b> the environmental consequences of food waste sent to a typical landfill.\n\nWhat actually happens to food waste in landfill, according to educators who explain this process to students, is considerably less harmless than most students initially imagine. <u>Food waste buried in a landfill decomposes without sufficient oxygen, a process that produces methane, a gas with a considerably stronger warming effect on the atmosphere than the carbon dioxide released when the same waste is composted properly instead.</u> This difference, educators argue, is precisely why diverting food waste to composting matters environmentally, not merely practically.\n\n[I] Some students initially assume that composting and simply throwing food waste away produce roughly the same environmental outcome either way. [II] Educators have increasingly used direct comparisons of methane versus composting emissions to correct this assumption. [III] This comparison appears to measurably increase how seriously students take correct waste sorting afterward. [IV] Educators consider this increased seriousness one of the programme's most valuable, if initially unexpected, outcomes.\n\nCritics occasionally argue that a single school's composting programme is too small in scale to meaningfully affect overall emissions. Educators generally acknowledge this limitation, while arguing that the programme's primary value lies in teaching habits and <b>awareness</b> that students are likely to carry into much larger decisions later in life."
