        "passage": "Students joining a riverbank clean-up for the first time often assume, based on how river pollution is typically depicted in photographs of obvious floating litter, that the rubbish they collect will consist mainly of clearly visible items like bottles and plastic bags. Environmental volunteers who have organised many such clean-ups consistently note that this assumption significantly <b>understates</b> how much of the actual collected weight comes from small, easily overlooked fragments rather than large, obvious items.\n\nWhat organisers have found when actually weighing and sorting collected material, according to volunteers who track these figures, is that small, broken-down plastic fragments, often barely noticeable individually, frequently make up a surprisingly large proportion of the total weight collected during a typical clean-up. <u>A single large plastic bottle is easy to spot and collect quickly, but the same riverbank may also contain hundreds of small fragments that individually seem negligible yet together outweigh the more obvious litter once all the small pieces are gathered and weighed.</u> This composition, volunteers argue, is precisely why thorough, careful searching matters more than simply collecting the most visible items quickly.\n\n[I] New volunteers sometimes focus mainly on large, easily visible litter during their first hour of a clean-up. [II] More experienced volunteers have increasingly demonstrated how to search more carefully for smaller fragments hidden among soil and vegetation. [III] This demonstration appears to measurably increase how much total rubbish newer volunteers collect by the end of a session. [IV] Organisers consider this increased thoroughness one of the clean-up's most valuable outcomes.\n\nCritics occasionally argue that collecting small plastic fragments by hand is too slow and inefficient to make a meaningful dent in river pollution overall. Organisers generally acknowledge this limitation, while arguing that raising <b>awareness</b> of how fragmented plastic pollution actually is may be just as valuable as the rubbish removed on a single day."
