        "passage": "Students bringing items to an e-waste collection drive for the first time often assume, based on how harmless an old, no-longer-working phone or charger looks sitting unused in a drawer, that such items pose no real risk if simply thrown into an ordinary rubbish bin. Recycling specialists who handle electronic waste consistently note that this assumption significantly <b>understates</b> the genuine environmental risk certain components inside these devices can pose once improperly discarded.\n\nWhat makes electronic waste genuinely different from ordinary household rubbish, according to specialists who process it, is the presence of specific chemical components, particularly within batteries, that can leach into soil and groundwater if a device ends up in a landfill not designed to contain such materials safely. <u>A battery that appears completely inert while sitting in a drawer can, once its casing corrodes inside an ordinary landfill, release chemicals capable of contaminating groundwater across a considerably wider area than the battery's own small size might suggest.</u> This disproportionate risk, specialists argue, is precisely why e-waste requires separate collection and specialised processing.\n\n[I] Some students initially see little difference between throwing away an old phone and throwing away an empty food container. [II] Specialists have increasingly used direct comparisons of battery contamination risk to correct this assumption. [III] This comparison appears to measurably increase how seriously students take proper e-waste disposal afterward. [IV] Specialists consider this increased seriousness one of the drive's most valuable outcomes.\n\nCritics occasionally argue that a single school collection drive is too small in scale to meaningfully affect overall e-waste contamination. Specialists generally acknowledge this limitation, while arguing that the disposal habits students learn through such drives are <b>transferable</b> to decisions they will make about their own electronics for the rest of their lives."
