        "passage": "Ecotourism is travel to natural areas that protects the environment and improves the <b>well-being</b> of local people. Unlike mass tourism, which often focuses on large hotels and crowded attractions, it encourages small groups, local guides and activities that have a low impact on nature. Viet Nam, with its national parks, mangrove forests and mountain villages, offers many opportunities for this kind of travel.\n\nIn a mountain village, for example, visitors may stay in a local house, walk to waterfalls with a guide and learn to cook traditional dishes. The villagers earn money without leaving their homes, and they have a reason to protect the forests around them. <u>When people see that a healthy environment brings them income, they are far more likely to look after it.</u> Some communities have also banned hunting and tree-cutting in areas that tourists visit.\n\n[I] Ecotourism does, however, have limits. [II] Fragile places can be damaged if too many visitors arrive at the same time. [III] Some companies use the word \"eco\" only for advertising, while their tours cause as much harm as ordinary ones. [IV] Without careful control, income may also go mainly to outside companies instead of to local residents.\n\nTourists can make a difference too. They can choose operators that employ local guides, avoid buying products made from wild animals and take their rubbish with them. When travellers behave responsibly, <b>they</b> show that enjoying nature and protecting it can go together."
