        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "Watching wild animals in their natural habitats is one of the most popular forms of ecotourism. Visitors may see dolphins from boats, observe birds in wetlands or walk through forests to look for monkeys. Such experiences can be unforgettable, and they often inspire people to care more about nature.\n\nHowever, the presence of tourists can disturb animals. Boats that approach too closely may frighten dolphins, and loud noises can cause birds to abandon their nests. Animals that are fed by visitors may lose their natural fear of humans, which can <b>endanger</b> them when they come near roads or villages. <u>For this reason, responsible operators teach visitors to keep a safe distance and to watch quietly.</u>\n\nWell-run wildlife tours also protect habitats. Guides explain why animals should not be fed, groups are kept small, and part of the income pays for monitoring and conservation work. Tourists who follow these rules often see more natural behaviour than <b>those</b> who rush towards the animals. Some tours even involve visitors in simple research, such as counting birds or recording the sounds of frogs.\n\nNot all wildlife attractions deserve support, however. Places that keep wild animals in small cages or force them to perform tricks are <b>exploiting</b> them for profit. Travellers should ask how the animals are treated and avoid attractions that allow visitors to touch or ride them. By choosing carefully, tourists can encourage businesses that respect wildlife."
