        "passage": "Whale watching has grown from a small activity into a global industry that attracts millions of people each year. Visitors travel to coastal waters in the hope of seeing these giant mammals leap, dive or <b>glide</b> beside their boats. For many coastal towns, the business has brought jobs and income that were once provided only by fishing.\n\nWhen it is well managed, whale watching can help to protect whales. Tour operators teach passengers about the animals' habits and the dangers they face, and many of them donate part of their profits to conservation projects. <u>People who have seen a whale in the wild are more likely to support laws that protect the oceans than those who have only read about them.</u> Scientists also benefit, as guides often share photographs and observations that help to track whale populations.\n\n[I] Yet the activity can harm the animals it is meant to celebrate. [II] Boats that approach too closely or too quickly may disturb mothers feeding their young. [III] Noise from engines can interfere with the sounds whales use to communicate and find food. [IV] In some regions, the animals have been seen to change their behaviour or leave areas where traffic is heavy.\n\nTo reduce the risks, many countries have introduced rules on how near boats may come to whales and how long they may stay. Responsible operators follow these rules even when no one is watching, and <b>they</b> encourage passengers to choose companies with a good record. In the long run, such an approach is likely to be good for both whales and the people who earn a living from them."
