        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Most travellers believe that they are respectful guests, yet complaints about tourist behaviour are increasing in many popular destinations. Residents describe visitors who leave rubbish on beaches, climb on ancient monuments or take photographs inside private homes. Such behaviour is often not intended to cause harm; rather, it results from a lack of <b>awareness</b>.\n\nPsychologists point out that people on holiday often behave differently from the way they do at home. Away from the people who know them, tourists may feel freed from the usual rules, and they may think that a few small mistakes will make no difference. <u>When many visitors reason in the same way, however, the combined effect can be serious damage to fragile places.</u>\n\nEducation can help. Some destinations now give visitors simple guidelines when they arrive, explaining which customs to respect and how to dispose of waste. Studies show that clear, friendly messages are far more effective than <b>those</b> that simply forbid certain actions, because people respond better to reasons than to orders. Local guides can also play a role by explaining why a particular site matters to the community.\n\nIn the end, responsible tourism depends on attitude. Travellers who take time to learn about a place before they arrive usually behave more <b>considerately</b> and enjoy richer experiences, since they understand what they are seeing. Respect, after all, is the best souvenir a visitor can leave behind."
