        "passage": "A student on the mangrove eco-trip initially found the slow boat journey through the narrow channels somewhat tedious, having expected a more dramatic, fast-paced adventure similar to trips she had seen in travel videos online. Her mood shifted considerably, however, when the guide pointed out a well-camouflaged bird species nestled among the roots that she would have completely missed on her own, leaving her feeling genuinely <b>captivated</b> for the remainder of the journey.\n\n<u>Rather than continuing to scan the horizon for something dramatic as she had during the trip's first half, she began deliberately watching the water close to the boat and the spaces directly among the roots, following the guide's suggestion about where wildlife actually tends to hide.</u> This shift in where she focused her attention changed what she noticed for the rest of the trip entirely.\n\nHer final observation notes were noticeably more <b>detailed</b> than her notes from the trip's first half, recording several species she would have missed entirely had she continued scanning the horizon instead. Her teacher specifically praised the shift in her attention as the key difference between her two sets of notes.\n\nShe now encourages other students visiting a mangrove forest for the first time not to expect dramatic, fast-paced scenery, suggesting that looking closely at the water and roots immediately around the boat usually reveals far more than scanning the wider horizon ever does."
