        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "Students joining a homestay tourism programme for the first time often assume, based on how most tourist experiences are typically structured around guided sightseeing, that a homestay similarly involves observing a host family's daily life from a comfortable, largely passive distance. Tourism researchers who study community-based homestay programmes consistently note that this assumption significantly <b>understates</b> how much of a homestay's actual value depends on direct, hands-on participation rather than observation alone.\n\nWhat distinguishes a genuinely successful homestay experience, according to researchers who study these programmes, is less the novelty of the setting than the visitor's willingness to participate directly in ordinary daily tasks alongside the host family, from cooking to simple farm work. <u>A visitor who merely watches a host family prepare a traditional meal gains considerably less insight into that family's actual daily life than one who participates directly in the preparation, however unskilled their initial attempts at the task might be.</u> This distinction, researchers argue, is precisely why well-designed homestay programmes actively encourage participation rather than simply arranging comfortable observation.\n\n[I] Some visitors initially feel hesitant to participate directly, worried about making mistakes in an unfamiliar household task. [II] Experienced host families have increasingly found ways to make this initial participation feel welcoming rather than intimidating for new guests. [III] This welcoming approach appears to measurably increase how willing visitors become to participate fully during the remainder of their stay. [IV] Researchers consider this increased willingness one of the most significant factors in a homestay's overall success.\n\nCritics occasionally argue that encouraging tourist participation in daily household tasks risks turning a family's genuine way of life into a kind of performance for visitors. Researchers generally acknowledge this risk, while arguing that a well-designed programme can make participation feel <b>genuine</b> rather than staged, by keeping group sizes small and avoiding scripted, repetitive activities."
