        "passage": "Visitors snorkelling over a coral reef for the first time often assume, based on coral's hard, rock-like visual appearance, that it can withstand brief casual contact, such as an accidental kick or a hand placed against it for balance, without any lasting consequence. Marine biologists who study reef ecosystems consistently note that this assumption significantly <b>understates</b> how delicate the living coral organism actually is beneath its sturdy-looking exterior.\n\nWhat makes coral genuinely vulnerable to contact, according to marine biologists who study reef damage, is that the hard structure visitors see and touch is actually a thin, living layer covering a skeleton built up slowly over many years. <u>A single careless touch can damage this thin living layer in a way that takes the coral colony considerably longer to repair than the moment of contact itself might suggest, sometimes leaving the affected coral permanently weakened even if it eventually recovers some of its original structure.</u> This slow-recovery characteristic, biologists argue, is precisely why even seemingly minor, brief contact carries disproportionate long-term consequences.\n\n[I] Some visitors initially assume a quick, gentle touch causes no meaningful harm compared with a forceful kick. [II] Marine biologists have increasingly explained to visitors that even gentle contact can damage coral's thin living layer. [III] This explanation appears to measurably reduce how often visitors touch coral at all during guided snorkelling trips. [IV] Biologists consider this reduction one of the more effective outcomes of pre-trip briefings on reef safety.\n\nCritics occasionally argue that strict no-touch rules are overly cautious given how briefly most visitors actually make contact with any coral. Marine biologists generally respond that even brief contact can cause disproportionate, <b>lasting</b> damage given how slowly coral actually recovers, making strict rules a reasonable precaution rather than excessive caution."
