        "passage": "Visitors exploring a limestone cave for the first time often assume, based on how dramatic and visually striking many cave formations appear, that such impressive shapes must have formed relatively quickly on a timescale comparable to other natural processes they are more familiar with. Geologists who study cave systems consistently note that this assumption significantly <b>understates</b> just how extraordinarily slow the actual process of formation truly is.\n\nWhat makes cave formations genuinely remarkable, according to geologists who study them, is not primarily their visual drama but the almost unimaginably slow timescale over which mineral-rich water deposits the tiny amounts of material that eventually accumulate into a stalactite or stalagmite. <u>A single stalactite that appears substantial to a visitor's eye may represent only a few centimetres of actual growth accumulated over several centuries, meaning the formation a visitor observes in a few minutes took far longer to form than any human lifetime could span.</u> This mismatch between a brief human encounter and the formation's true timescale, geologists argue, is precisely what makes careful conservation so important.\n\n[I] Some visitors initially assume that touching a formation causes no lasting harm given how solid and permanent it appears. [II] Geologists have increasingly explained that oils from human skin can coat a formation's surface and halt its further growth entirely. [III] This explanation appears to measurably increase how strictly visitors follow no-touching guidelines during guided tours. [IV] Geologists consider this increased compliance one of the more effective outcomes of pre-tour safety and conservation briefings.\n\nCritics occasionally argue that strict no-touch rules seem excessive for formations that have already survived thousands of years of natural conditions. Geologists generally respond that a formation's long survival under natural conditions does not mean it can withstand the specific, concentrated damage caused by <b>repeated</b> human contact from thousands of annual visitors."
