        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "Visitors touring a floating village for the first time often assume, based on how unfamiliar life on water appears compared with their own land-based routines, that such a community represents a simplified or less convenient alternative to living permanently on solid ground. Anthropologists who study floating communities consistently note that this assumption significantly <b>overlooks</b> how deliberately and precisely such communities have adapted their entire way of life to suit their specific watery environment.\n\nWhat makes a floating community's design genuinely sophisticated, according to anthropologists who study these settlements, is that nearly every structure, from houses to schools to shops, has been specifically engineered to solve a practical problem unique to living on water rather than simply imitating an equivalent structure found on land. <u>A floating house's platform is built to rise and fall naturally with changing water levels, a design feature that a land-based house would never need but that is absolutely essential for a structure meant to remain stable and usable through an entire river's seasonal flooding cycle.</u> This purpose-built adaptation, anthropologists argue, reflects considerable accumulated practical knowledge rather than a simplified compromise.\n\n[I] Visitors sometimes view a floating school or floating shop as a slightly awkward improvisation compared with an equivalent building on land. [II] Anthropologists have increasingly highlighted how precisely such structures are engineered for their specific watery context. [III] This emphasis appears to measurably shift how visitors describe the community afterward, from viewing it as improvised toward recognising it as genuinely well-adapted. [IV] Guides consider this shift in perspective one of the tour's most valuable outcomes.\n\nCritics occasionally argue that increased tourism to floating villages risks disrupting residents' genuine daily routines for the sake of visitor curiosity. Guides generally acknowledge this tension, while arguing that tours explaining the community's actual adaptations, rather than presenting it as a curiosity, can foster <b>genuine</b> respect for the residents' practical knowledge."
