        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Visitors watching small migratory birds at a local wetland reserve for the first time often assume, based on the bird's modest size and the reserve's seemingly ordinary local setting, that such a small creature likely stays within a relatively limited surrounding area rather than undertaking any truly extensive journey. Ornithologists who study bird migration consistently note that this assumption significantly <b>understates</b> the extraordinary distances many migratory species actually cover each year to reach a single wetland reserve.\n\nWhat makes migratory bird journeys genuinely remarkable, according to ornithologists who track these movements, is the sheer distance a bird weighing only a few dozen grams can cover using little more than its own body's stored energy and favourable wind patterns. <u>Certain migratory species documented at reserves like this one are known to travel several thousand kilometres between their breeding grounds and wintering areas, a journey that can span multiple countries and even continents within a single migratory season.</u> This scale of travel, ornithologists argue, makes a wetland reserve considerably more significant than its modest local appearance might suggest to an unfamiliar visitor.\n\n[I] Some visitors initially view a small bird spotted at the reserve as simply a local resident species rather than a long-distance traveller. [II] Guides have increasingly shared specific migration distance data to correct this assumption directly during tours. [III] This information appears to measurably increase how much visitors value protecting the reserve's habitat afterward. [IV] Guides consider this increased sense of value one of the trip's most significant outcomes.\n\nCritics occasionally argue that a single local wetland reserve is too small to meaningfully support a migratory species' survival across its entire range. Ornithologists generally acknowledge this limitation, while arguing that a chain of such reserves across a migratory route provides <b>essential</b> stopover habitat without which the overall journey would become considerably more difficult for the species to complete."
