        "passage": "Visitors watching a sea turtle nest for the first time often assume, based on the hatchling's tiny size and the beach's seemingly calm and ordinary setting, that the short dash from nest to sea is a brief and largely safe journey requiring little real protection. Marine biologists who study nesting beaches consistently note that this assumption significantly <b>understates</b> how dangerous the short crawl actually is for a newly hatched turtle.\n\nWhat makes a hatchling's journey genuinely perilous, according to biologists who track nest survival rates, is the sheer number of threats a creature weighing only a few dozen grams must cross open sand to avoid. <u>Ghost crabs, seabirds and, increasingly, artificial lighting from nearby buildings are all documented causes of hatchlings being caught or disorientated before they ever reach the water, a danger that can affect an entire nest within minutes of hatching.</u> This scale of risk, biologists argue, makes a single nesting beach considerably more fragile than its calm appearance might suggest to an unfamiliar visitor.\n\n[I] Some visitors initially view a hatchling's short crawl as simply a natural event needing no outside help. [II] Rangers have increasingly shared specific survival-rate data to correct this assumption directly during patrols. [III] This information appears to measurably increase how carefully visitors behave around marked nesting sites afterward. [IV] Rangers consider this increased sense of care one of the patrol's most significant outcomes.\n\nCritics occasionally argue that a single beach patrol is too small to meaningfully affect a species' survival across an entire coastline. Biologists generally acknowledge this limitation, while arguing that a chain of such patrols along a nesting range provides <b>essential</b> protection without which far fewer hatchlings would complete the journey to the sea at all."
